    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5518986/casual-hire-special-advisor-to-uh-athletics": {
      "title": "Casual Hire - Special Advisor to UH Athletics",
      "description": "Casual Hire - Special Advisor to UH Athletics Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02223 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/15/2026 Closing Date 10/18/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire - Special Advisor to UH Athletics Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Date Posted: October 15, 2026 Closing Date: October 18, 2026 Full Time/ Part Time: Temporary, Part Time Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offer no benefits. Hours may vary The Special Advisor to University of Hawai‘i Athletics (SA) will serve as a trusted advisor, ambassador, and resource to the department and baseball program. SA will work directly with the Director of Athletics and department senior leadership team on special projects, including community relations, alumni connections, and fundraising. SA will also serve as a resource to the Head Coach and baseball staff, providing guidance and perspective in the areas of recruiting, player development, alumni engagement, and community outreach, while helping strengthen the program's profile. Duties and Responsibilities: Baseball Recruiting & Program Development Assist the coaching staff with recruiting efforts by leveraging professional and collegiate baseball relationships and networks. Subject to NCAA rules, participate in select recruiting events, prospect evaluations, campus visits, and relationship-building activities. Serve as an ambassador for the program and communicate its vision, values, academic opportunities, and player-development philosophy to prospective student-athletes and their families. Baseball Player Development & Mentorship Provide perspective and mentorship to student-athletes based on professional playing experience. Subject to NCAA rules, participate in team meetings, practices, player-development discussions, and other activities as appropriate. Share insights regarding preparation, leadership, professionalism, mental approach, clubhouse culture, and the transition from college baseball to professional baseball. Department Alumni Relations & Engagement Help strengthen relationships between the department and its alumni. Assist in developing alumni events, communications, networking opportunities, and other engagement initiatives. Encourage former players and supporters to remain connected to the program and current student-athletes. Community Outreach & Program Ambassadorship Represent the department at select community, alumni, fundraising, and charitable events. Attend events, meetings, meals with donors and potential donors to share insights and build relationships to further UH Athletics’ fundraising goals. Assist with youth baseball clinics, speaking engagements, camps, and community initiatives. Help build relationships with local baseball organizations, schools, businesses, civic leaders, and community partners. Promote the values, traditions, and positive impact of the program. Other Duties: *Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawai‘i policies. *Know, recognize, and comply with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Bachelors Degree or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Former college and/or professional baseball player experience Strong relationships within collegiate, professional, and amateur baseball. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and relationship-building skills. Demonstrated commitment to mentoring young people and supporting student-athlete development. Desirable Qualifications Alumni status or demonstrated connection to the college and baseball program. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries: Lois Manin, Deputy AD – Internal Operations, manin@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5518985/educational-specialist-0097630t": {
      "title": "Educational Specialist (0097630T)",
      "description": "Educational Specialist (0097630T) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02221 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Opening Date 10/15/2026 Closing Date 10/29/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097630T Description Title: Educational Specialist Position Number: 0097630T Hiring Unit: College of Education / Associate Dean of Academic Affairs Location: Manoa Date Posted: October 15, 2026 Closing date: October 29, 2026 Band: B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Special funds. Appointment to begin November 2026 or soon thereafter. Renewal contingent upon satisfactory performance, department needs, and availability of funds. The selectee must successfully pass a criminal background check and fulfill all mandatory training and compliance requirements. Duties and Responsibilities: * Conceives, plans, and directs the development and implementation of recruitment, outreach, and communication plans targeting emergency hires and other Hawaii Department of Education (HIDOE) personnel (e.g., Educational Assistants (EA), substitute teachers, and Part-time Teachers (PTT)) statewide, in collaboration with the COE Associate Dean and COE Recruitment Specialist. Develops and maintains relationships with HIDOE administrators and leadership to identify and monitor the evolving needs of the teaching workforce in Hawai`i. * Maintain familiarity with COE teacher preparation programs at the post-baccalaureate and master's degree levels to provide information and support to prospective students on program selection, fit and admissibility. Develops and disseminates marketing and informational materials promoting COE's post-baccalaureate certificate teacher preparation programs through electronic, print, and social media channels to reach HIDOE personnel and the general public. * Advise and counsel prospective post-baccalaureate students, through in-person, telephone, and email communications, regarding admission requirements and processes, financial aid and stipend opportunities, COE's programs and services, and pathways and requirements for obtaining teacher licensure. Advise and guide applicants throughout the enrollment process, maintaining ongoing communication to support application completion, resolve questions or barriers, and facilitate program admission. * Manage, monitor, analyze, and report post-baccalaureate certificate program application and admission data; evaluate trends and outcomes to inform and enhance recruitment and admissions strategies for targeted populations. * Represent the College at statewide recruitment events to promote post-baccalaureate certificate programs and attract a highly qualified and diverse applicant pool; establish and strengthen recruitment pipelines and cultivate strategic partnerships with HIDOE stakeholders. Performs other duties as assigned. * Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in education or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for student support and/or project management; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of student support and/or project management as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with student support and/or project management. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. K-12 teaching experience. Ability to travel to school/project sites, including neighbor islands, and work occasional evenings or weekends for project-related activities. Ability to manage multiple priorities, organize workload, meet deadlines, and perform effectively in a high-paced environment. Ability to exercise tact, diplomacy, initiative, confidentiality, and sound judgment when working with project personnel, school/community partners, and sensitive information. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (Class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation that meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications: Teacher preparation and/or experiences in Education. Knowledge of Hawaii teacher licensure requirements. Knowledge of COE teacher licensure programs. Experience as a Hawaii classroom teacher. Professional experience in marketing, enrollment management, or recruitment. Background in collecting, analyzing, and preparing comprehensive reports and executive summaries. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents:1. cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2. names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references, 3. resume, and 4. official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire). Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Cecily Ornelles, cecily@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5518798/instructor-i2-atmospheric-sciences-0086092t": {
      "title": "Instructor (I2) Atmospheric Sciences (#0086092T)",
      "description": "Instructor (I2) Atmospheric Sciences (#0086092T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - SOEST, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02214 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Ocean and Earth Science and Technology Division Atmospheric Sciences Opening Date 10/15/2026 Position Number: 0086092T Description Title: Instructor (I2) Atmospheric Sciences (#0086092T) Position Number: 86092T Hiring Unit: Department of Atmospheric Sciences, School of Ocean and Earth Science and Technology Location: Manoa campus Date Posted: 10/15/2026 Closing Date: continuous Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-month Tenure Track: Non-Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time, 100% FTE Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: This is a temporary, non-tenure track, full-time, 9-month appointment, to begin January 2027, or as mutually agreed. Pending position clearance and availability of funds. Review of applications will begin on November 20th, 2026 and will continue open until the position is filled. Extension of position is dependent upon availability of funding, program needs, and satisfactory performance. Possibility of conversion to permanent. Duties and Responsibilities Develop teaching/educational programs in atmospheric physics and dynamics which include classroom instruction and contributions to undergraduate programs. Teach core undergraduate courses in atmospheric thermodynamics and dynamics Develop new undergraduate courses in climate science and its societal applications Contribute to the Climate Science and Society Certificate and future Applied Climate Science degree pathway Participate in undergraduate advising, curriculum development, and departmental service. Minimum Qualifications A Master's degree from a college or university of recognized standing in atmospheric sciences, oceanography, chemistry, or in another related field, with the major work in the field in which the instructional assignment is made or duties performed; Evidence of continued professional growth, skill, and knowledge (e.g. additional coursework, certificates, or other educational activities that advance knowledge of their discipline and/or work with students). Evidence of increasing proficiency in ability to teach effectively and/or to provide clinical supervision. Evidence of increasing proficiency in academic advising. Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Demonstrated scholarly achievement. Desirable Qualifications Ph.D. degree in atmospheric sciences, oceanography, chemistry, or in another related field Expertise in one or more of the following areas: atmospheric dynamics, atmospheric thermodynamics, weather analysis and forecasting, climate science, climate impacts and adaptation, Earth system science, or climate applications. Experience teaching undergraduate atmospheric or climate science courses, including mathematically rigorous core courses. Demonstrated ability to teach atmospheric thermodynamics, atmospheric dynamics, and related quantitative undergraduate courses. Experience developing innovative undergraduate curricula, particularly interdisciplinary courses that connect climate science with societal applications . Demonstrated ability to communicate climate science effectively to students from diverse academic backgrounds and interests . Experience mentoring undergraduate students and contributing to student success. Interest in supporting the development of new undergraduate programs, including the Climate Science and Society Certificate and future Applied Climate Science degree pathway . To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: A cover letter in which you identify the position to which you are applying; Curriculum vitae; Statements of teaching interests; Names and contact information of three individuals willing to provide professional references Transcripts with confidential identifiable information (i.e.social security number, birthdate, etc) redacted. (Copies acceptable with application, official transcripts will be required at the time of hire.) Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Jennifer Griswold, 808-956-8775 metdept@hawaii.ed u Fax 956-2877 EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5517297/program-specialist-0097726t": {
      "title": "Program Specialist (0097726T)",
      "description": "Program Specialist (0097726T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02209 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - CALL - Office of the Dean Opening Date 10/15/2026 Closing Date 10/29/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097726T Description Title: Program Specialist Position Number: 0097726T Hiring Unit: Uehiro Program, College of Arts, Languages & Letters Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa Date Posted: October 15, 2026 Closing Date: October 29, 2026 Band: A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities: *Under the direction and guidance of the Director for Uehiro Program (Program), perform a wide variety of professional administrative duties to support the daily operation of the Program. *Serve as the primary operational point of contact for routine Program matters; answer or appropriately route Program inquiries from students, faculty, graduate assistants, University offices, and external partners. *Draft and manage professional correspondence in English and Japanese, including announcements, meeting materials, schedules, and follow-up. *Maintain an integrated calendar and tracking system for deadlines, events, award cycles, meetings, and reporting tasks; identify missing information or delays and initiate routine follow-up. *Carry out the Director's priorities, resolve routine scheduling, documentation, and coordination issues, and refer academic, policy, personnel, fiscal, or institutional matters to the Director. *Prepare agendas, briefing notes, meeting summaries, action lists, and concise status summaries for Director review. *Coordinate the Program's research, higher-education, outreach, and community-building initiatives, including p4c and other K-12 and school-based activities, involving Okinawa, Japan, East Asia, and the broader Asia-Pacific region, with particular attention to the Program's sustained Okinawa-related activities. *Support institutional exchanges, collaborative projects, visiting students and student groups, scholars and delegations, community relationships, and other partnerships by coordinating communications, meetings, itineraries, materials, logistics, and follow-up. *Maintain clear and culturally appropriate English-Japanese communication with collaborators including partner institutions and community groups. *Provide end-to-end coordination of approved conferences, lectures, workshops, symposia, cultural programs, public events and community initiatives from initial planning and setup through on-site execution and post-event evaluation. *Arrange event logistics, securing venues, travel arrangements, registration, guest hospitality, publicity, technical support, and vendor contracts to ensure seamless execution. *Serve as the primary operational contact for speakers, participants, students, volunteers, community participants, and partner organizations, ensuring task completion and delivering real-time support during events. *Administer the operational processes for the CALL Uehiro Dissertation Fellowship, student travel support, and graduate assistantships. *Process student applications, respond to applicant questions, collect evaluations, verify completeness, and organize records for selection. *Draft award communications, coordinate payment and appointment documents with University offices, monitor outstanding requirements, and collect recipient reports. *Maintain accurate and confidential student-support records. *Maintain organized Program files, contact lists, project histories, award records, reports, forms, correspondence, and digital archives. *Coordinate routine updates to the Program website and public communication materials in accordance with approved content priorities, permissions, and privacy requirements. *Maintain partner project histories, prepare summaries and route proposed commitments, agreements, or expenditures for approval by the director. *Gather data and narrative information to prepare bilingual summaries, annual-report content, and presentations. *Collect photos, attendance data, evaluations, and other documents for Program recording and reporting. Coordinate task assignments and work assignments for team members or student helpers as directed. Other duties as assigned. * Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Education, Business Administration, Arts and Sciences, International Relations, or related field, and 1year of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for academic program coordination, event planning or administrative support or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of academic program coordination, event planning and/or administrative support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with academic program coordination, event planning and/or administrative support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of academic program coordination, event planning and/or administrative support. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and use online collaboration, shared calendar, and presentation software. Professional working proficiency in spoken and written English and Japanese to independently draft business materials, lead meetings, and communicate with external partners. Understanding and applying Japanese cultural practices/norms in academic and other social contexts. Demonstrated ability to effectively communicate with a variety of stakeholders and to coordinate multiple tasks while meeting required deadlines. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain collaborative working relationships with faculty, students, community partners, and other stakeholders in support of program objectives. Demonstrated ability to coordinate meetings, events, or program activities, including scheduling, logistics, and communications. Ability and willingness to work occasional evenings or weekends and to travel when required for Program events and activities. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications: Graduate degree in Education, Philosophy, Asian Studies, International Education, or a closely related field. Experience supporting a university-based research, education, outreach, or international programs. Experience with Okinawa and Japan within their East Asian and Asia-Pacific contexts, including cultural, historical, educational, institutional and collaborative contexts relevant to research, education, and community engagement. Experience administering program operations, including coordinating application or selection processes, maintaining records, and communicating with participants. Experience preparing, translating, or editing professional or academic materials in English and Japanese. Experience with website content management, online forms, digital communications, reporting, or digital archiving. Familiarity with University of Hawai'i administrative procedures or comparable higher-education systems. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum, supplemental minimum, and desirable qualifications. Current resume. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three professional references. Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please redact references to Social Security numbers and birthdates on transcript copies. All requested documents/information will become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the hire date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Masato Ishida, masatoi@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5481780/research-associate-regulatory-associate-0097177t-readvertisement": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) - (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT",
      "description": "Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) - (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - UH Cancer Center, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01969 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Opening Date 10/16/2026 Closing Date 10/26/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097177T Description Benefits Description Title: Research Associate (Regulatory Associate) – (0097177T) - READVERTISEMENT Position Number: 0097177T Hiring Unit: RES & DEAN OF GRAD DIV, CANCER CT HI, CANCER CT HI Location: Honolulu, Kak'ako, Hawai'i Date Posted: October 16, 2026 Closing Date: October 26, 2026 Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBA, minimum $4,627 Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities: *Serves as the Regulatory Associate for the Clinical Trials Office (CTO), conducting, reviewing, and submitting clinical research studies through the study start-up process by preparing initial clinical research study applications and patient consent forms for submission to the respective Internal Review Board (IRBs) for local site activation of national cooperative group trials, pharmaceutical- sponsored trials, and investigator-initiated trials within the UH Cancer Center clinical trials network. Review all approved consents before studies are officially activated. *Maintains sponsor-written consent forms, log of local IRB reviews, protocol regulatory files of all IRB submissions and approvals, annual continuation renewal applications, sponsor amendments, and responds to IRB stipulations and inquiries to ensure document language is in compliance. Acts as a liaison with multiple IRBs. *Maintains data integrity by entering information accurately and timely in the Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS). Ensures appropriate research credentials are tracked and kept current for CTO staff, Translational Cancer Research (TCR) faculty and staff, and community investigators and staff. *Assist the Regulatory Supervisor with developing regulatory-related Clinical Research Regulatory Associate standard operating procedures, training, and documentation. *Assists frontline troubleshooting for all Hawaii Cancer Consortium/Network (i.e., community hospitals and clinics) staff at partnering institutions on regulatory-related issues. *Facilitates the review of Serious Adverse Events (SAEs) with research staff and PIs and communicates relevant SAE reports to IRBs. *Completes and maintains Delegation Task Logs when required by Sponsors and is responsible for regulatory duties on all ongoing clinical trials. Monitors the status of sponsor-issued protocol changes. *Contact study sponsors for assistance or seek additional information from the CTO Clinical Manager, principal investigators (PIs) and co-PIs, and other investigators regarding IRB queries. *Collaborates with CTO Clinical Manager, PIs, co-PIs, local PIs, lead Clinical Research Associates (CRAs), Compliance, Biocore, Investigator Initiative Trials (IIT) Manager, Protocol Coordinator, CTO Department Managers and Administrative staff, and other investigators for all IRB issues. *Partner with Compliance to maintain credentialing, audit preparation, and review of regulatory documents. *Works closely with UH HSR (Human Subjects Research), WIRB-Copernicus Group (WCG), Central Institutional Review Board, any other relevant IRBs, Office of Compliance, and IRB personnel from other Cancer Center programs, and Hawai`i Cancer Consortium institutions. *Communicates with the CTMS Manager and team for database changes, data quality assurance, and other CTMS tasks for accuracy to generate Clinical Research Regulatory Associate reports of clinical trial enrollments as mandated by the National Cancer Institute (NCI) and other sponsors. *Assists with maintaining an appropriate tracking system in CTMS for all protocol amendments/revisions and annual continuation renewals to ensure all federal regulations are met within the required parameters. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in health or related field and zero (0) year of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for clinical research; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the areas of practical and potential value of health care research, research process and regulatory requirements (Human Subject protection, conflict of interest, research misconduct) and working knowledge of medical terminology demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Functional knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with Clinical Research, regulatory protection program, regulatory procedures, HIPPA Compliance and regulations for research on human subjects. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of Clinical regulatory, Institutional review board (IRB) protocols, Clinical trials management systems (CTMS), and Human Subjects. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply work processing software, interpret informed consent forms and Google Suite, Microsoft Office and Adobe Acrobat. Supplemental Qualifications: Familiar with clinical trials and informed consent process. Desirable Qualifications: Demonstrated ability to work independently and take initiative on various projects. General knowledge of the University of Hawaii (UH) regulatory systems, research grants, and preparation of proposals and reports. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2) resume, 3) names and contact information of three (3) professional references and 4) official transcripts (OR original documentation from the institution confirming degree OR copy of U.S. board certification). Copy of transcript is acceptable in place of U.S. board certification for evaluation purposes but original is required upon hire. Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA will be required before date of hire. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant (Note: Only complete applications will be considered.) NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: hr@cc.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Address 701 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5518244/casual-hire-program-coordinator": {
      "title": "(Casual Hire) Program Coordinator",
      "description": "(Casual Hire) Program Coordinator Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - College of Enginrg, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02211 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - CoE - Electrical & Computer Engineering Opening Date 10/19/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: *****Causal Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai’i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative professional and technical (APT) or Instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees and up to one calendar year for part-time employees. Title: (Casual Hire) Program Coordinator for NRT Program Position Number: *****Casual Hire Hiring Unit: College of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Manoa Date Posted: 10/19/2026 Closing Date: continuous Salary: $29 per hour Schedule: Up to 19 per week Full Time/Part Time: Part Time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities DESCARTES is a National Science Foundation Research Traineeship (NRT) award, working on core AI research and on preparing the next generation of workforce for an AI-infused workplace. The Program Coordinator (PC) will be the central administrative role for the DESCARTES collaboration. This role provides assistance to various components related to graduate students, faculty, curriculum and implementation of the NRT program. The PC must adapt to changing needs as the program evolves. In the lifetime of the grant, about 60 students will be trainees at various points. The primary responsibility is administrative implementation of program goals with NRT faculty, and finding strategies and mechanisms for executing these goals in the framework of University of Hawaii (UH) regulations. In the process, the PC builds a network within UH to ensure smooth functioning and advance the goals of the grant. Duties change as the project evolves, and includes (but not limited to): 1. Provide administrative support for NRT activities and goals, including fiscal support specific to this grant; 2. Work with faculty, students and administrative personnel to assess, record, document, and disseminate research and teaching activities, and showcase them for open houses, public dissemination and reporting; 3. Organize events and activities related to the working, publicity and dissemination activities of the NRT grant; 4. Help with recruitment activities related to the grant, including but not limited to production, publication and advertising of graduate student brochures; 5. Travel or remotely attend NRT annual meetings as well as PC meetings, representing our program in the PC capacity; 6. Help with administrative support on new proposals spun off the program; 7. Coordinate with other stakeholders for social media outreach; 8. Work with administrative and fiscal staff to ensure compliance with UH regulations; 9. Assist with reimbursements, procurements, payments, applications and requests related to NRT functioning as need arises. Candidates can expect a respectful and stimulating work environment with diverse stakeholders (students, faculty across multiple colleges in UH and from other NRT institutes). We are working to transform education to adapt to massive changes to come in the workforce due to the disruptive potential of AI, and we are looking for candidates who appreciate this potential and leverage this experience for their future endeavors. This is a casual hire position for 19 hours/week. The pay is $29/hour. While reasonable accommodations can be made, please note that this is not a remote working position. The appointment is one month from December 1, 2026 to December 31, 2026. Minimum Qualifications 1. Bachelor’s degree or equivalent from a college or university of recognized standing in business, engineering, economics, computer science or a related field. Exceptional undergraduates will also be considered if they are proficient in AI tools and agents. 2. One (1) year of professional experience in research and education program administration, grant writing, economic development, or other professional research or instruction. 3. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, attention to detail, writing skills and time management. 4. Comfort and willingness to learn basic software and tools, including AI tools and agents. 5. Interest and ability to understand ongoing research at an executive level. 6. Ability to manage and prioritize multiple deadlines and tasks. Desirable Qualifications 1. Master’s degree from a college or university of recognized standing in business, engineering, economics, computer science or a related field. 2. Experience and interest in teaching, research and training programs in STEM. 3. Experience preparing competitive grants and project proposals, preparing budgets, negotiating contracts and awards. 4. Knowledge of, and experience with, state and federal funding agencies and procedures. 5. Familiarity with the methods, principles, and techniques used in student recruitment and program assessment, including marketing methods, assessment and evaluation, and survey research. 6. Evidence of past success in working with research-oriented faculty in program development and implementation. 7. Familiarity with AI tools, desktop publishing, database management, and web content management. To Apply Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an online application through NeoGov Submit resume, cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum qualifications, names of 3 professional references, and official transcripts via the NEOGOV Online application portal. Note: If this is your first time applying for a position using NEOGOV, you will need to create a profile. Inquiries Yuanzhang Xiao, yxiao8@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5518332/casual-hire-program-coordinator": {
      "title": "(Casual Hire) Program Coordinator",
      "description": "(Casual Hire) Program Coordinator Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - College of Enginrg, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02212 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - CoE - Electrical & Computer Engineering Opening Date 10/19/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: *****Causal Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai’i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative professional and technical (APT) or Instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees and up to one calendar year for part-time employees. Title: (Casual Hire) Program Coordinator for NRT Program Position Number: *****Casual Hire Hiring Unit: College of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Manoa Date Posted: 10/19/2026 Closing Date: continuous Salary: $29 per hour Schedule: Up to 19 per week Full Time/Part Time: Part Time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities DESCARTES is a National Science Foundation Research Traineeship (NRT) award, working on core AI research and on preparing the next generation of workforce for an AI-infused workplace. The Program Coordinator (PC) will be the central administrative role for the DESCARTES collaboration. This role provides assistance to various components related to graduate students, faculty, curriculum and implementation of the NRT program. The PC must adapt to changing needs as the program evolves. In the lifetime of the grant, about 60 students will be trainees at various points. The primary responsibility is administrative implementation of program goals with NRT faculty, and finding strategies and mechanisms for executing these goals in the framework of University of Hawaii (UH) regulations. In the process, the PC builds a network within UH to ensure smooth functioning and advance the goals of the grant. Duties change as the project evolves, and includes (but not limited to): 1. Provide administrative support for NRT activities and goals, including fiscal support specific to this grant; 2. Work with faculty, students and administrative personnel to assess, record, document, and disseminate research and teaching activities, and showcase them for open houses, public dissemination and reporting; 3. Organize events and activities related to the working, publicity and dissemination activities of the NRT grant; 4. Help with recruitment activities related to the grant, including but not limited to production, publication and advertising of graduate student brochures; 5. Travel or remotely attend NRT annual meetings as well as PC meetings, representing our program in the PC capacity; 6. Help with administrative support on new proposals spun off the program; 7. Coordinate with other stakeholders for social media outreach; 8. Work with administrative and fiscal staff to ensure compliance with UH regulations; 9. Assist with reimbursements, procurements, payments, applications and requests related to NRT functioning as need arises. Candidates can expect a respectful and stimulating work environment with diverse stakeholders (students, faculty across multiple colleges in UH and from other NRT institutes). We are working to transform education to adapt to massive changes to come in the workforce due to the disruptive potential of AI, and we are looking for candidates who appreciate this potential and leverage this experience for their future endeavors. This is a casual hire position for 19 hours/week. The pay is 29$/hour. While reasonable accommodations can be made, please note that this is not a remote working position. The initial appointment is one year, subject to satisfactory progress in periodic reviews. Upon satisfactory evaluation at the end of one year, candidates will be invited to continue, up to the term of the NRT project (expected to end in July 2028 or 2029), with possible pay revisions subject to budget constraints. Minimum Qualifications 1. Bachelor’s degree or equivalent from a college or university of recognized standing in business, engineering, economics, computer science or a related field. Exceptional undergraduates will also be considered if they are proficient in AI tools and agents. 2. One (1) year of professional experience in research and education program administration, grant writing, economic development, or other professional research or instruction. 3. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, attention to detail, writing skills and time management. 4. Comfort and willingness to learn basic software and tools, including AI tools and agents. 5. Interest and ability to understand ongoing research at an executive level. 6. Ability to manage and prioritize multiple deadlines and tasks. Desirable Qualifications 7. Master’s degree from a college or university of recognized standing in business, engineering, economics, computer science or a related field. 8. Experience and interest in teaching, research and training programs in STEM. 9. Experience preparing competitive grants and project proposals, preparing budgets, negotiating contracts and awards. 10. Knowledge of, and experience with, state and federal funding agencies and procedures. 11. Familiarity with the methods, principles, and techniques used in student recruitment and program assessment, including marketing methods, assessment and evaluation, and survey research. 12. Evidence of past success in working with research-oriented faculty in program development and implementation. 13. Familiarity with AI tools, desktop publishing, database management, and web content management. To Apply Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an online application through NeoGov Submit resume, cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum qualifications, names of 3 professional references, and official transcripts via the NEOGOV Online application portal. Note: If this is your first time applying for a position using NEOGOV, you will need to create a profile. Inquiries Yuanzhang Xiao, yxiao8@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5523544/casual-hire-research-support-staff-ctahr-maui-county": {
      "title": "(Casual hire) Research Support Staff (CTAHR/Maui County)",
      "description": "(Casual hire) Research Support Staff (CTAHR/Maui County) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Kahului, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02257 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Maui County Opening Date 10/21/2026 Closing Date 11/4/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: Casual Description Benefits Questions Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai’i may apply for Casual Hire appointments, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits provided to regular Board of Regents or Civil Service employees. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees.**** Title : Research Support Staff Position Number: Casual Vacancies available: 2 Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Maui County Location: Maui County; Kahului, Maui, HI Date Posted : October 21, 2026 Closing Date : November 4, 2026, 11:59PM HST Salary : $26.00 - $30.00 per hour; salary commensurate with experience Full Time/Part Time : Full-time or part-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : These positions are subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. For best consideration, all application materials should be submitted by November 4, 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities *Coordinate technical logistics for setting up and maintaining field research for Maui County and/or laboratory trials evaluating crop performance, soil responses, and pest management systems. *Oversee scientific data logging systems and collect environmental parameters, soil metrics, water quality data, and plant growth measurements using scientific field instruments. *Coordinate the collection, cataloging, and preparation of soil, plant tissue, water, insects, and/or microorganism samples for analytical laboratory processing. *Perform technical field evaluations to monitor pest/disease incidence and report physiological crop conditions to research project leads. *Log experimental trial parameters, field mapping coordinates, and observation data into specialized research databases and web-based decision tools. *Oversee daily technical operations and environmental schedules for research which include greenhouses, hydroponic units, and/or field plots. *Program, troubleshoot, and calibrate automated irrigation networks, environmental control computers, and specialized research facility equipment. *Coordinate research plot layout schedules, treatment application timing, and technical equipment setup for research trials. Inspect research facilities, automated environmental controls, and field instrumentation to perform specialized technical maintenance and schedule major facility repairs. Maintain detailed operational logs, chemical/pesticide application tracking, inventory records, and facility usage schedules. Manage procurement logistics for specialized research supplies, fertilizers, field tools, and safety gear. Monitor field and facility compliance with state, federal, and University environmental health, safety, and worker protection standards. Assist research personnel in compiling trial summary tables, preparing field demonstration plot layouts, and drafting technical documentation. Transports equipment and materials for research. Perform other duties as assigned. *Denotes essential function Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in agriculture, agricultural engineering, plant and soil sciences or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for management of facilities, equipment and employees; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices & techniques in the area of agriculture demonstrated by knowledge, understanding & ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge & understanding of principles, theories, federal & state laws, rules, regulations & systems associated with agriculture. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes & resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in agriculture research. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish & maintain effective working relationships with internal & external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint. Ability of active listening skills and the ability to work independently across multiple locations, and flexibility to adapt to changing research and/or operational needs. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirements may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work outside of normal business, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and/or holidays as needed. Ability to lift, carry, or otherwise move supplies and equipment up to 40 pounds, with or without assistance or utilizing appropriate methods (e.g., carts, dollies, or team lifts). Desirable Qualifications Experience supporting agricultural research operations, field trial logistics, greenhouse operations, and/or biological research data collection (or equivalent combination of education and experience). Working knowledge of technical methods in agronomy, horticulture, and/or soil science, to include operation of controlled environment facilities, standard pest and/or disease identification, and/or scientific data logging and/or record-keeping procedures. Ability to coordinate technical field research tasks and maintain accurate data logs. Ability to operate and program automated environmental/irrigation systems. Ability to enforce safety protocols for chemical application and facility operation. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Laura Kahoohanohano, lkahooha@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits 01 Do you have a baccalaureate degree in agriculture, agricultural engineering, plant and soil sciences or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5509249/readvertisedpublic-safety-security-technology-specialist-0096925-extens": {
      "title": "***READVERTISED***Public Safety & Security Technology Specialist (0096925) Extension of Closing Date",
      "description": "***READVERTISED***Public Safety & Security Technology Specialist (0096925) Extension of Closing Date Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02145 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Office of Campus Services Division Department of Public Safety Opening Date 10/07/2026 Closing Date 11/4/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096925 Description ***READVERTISED*** Extension of Closing Date to 11/04/2026 Title: Public Safety and Security Technology Specialist (0096925) Position Number: 0096925 Hiring Unit: ADMINISTRATION, AUXIL ENT, CAMPUS SECURITY Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: 10/08/2026 Closing Date: 10/23/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions) *Serves as a Public Safety and Security Technology Specialist for University of Hawaii at Manoa (UHM) Department of Public Safety (DPS) by managing DPS security system and equipment including but not limited to specialized security systems (enterprise CCTV networks, IP cameras, digital/network video recorders, and radio communication hardware), Windows and Mac, workstations, tablets, and mobile devices. *Installs, configures, tests, and maintains security camera systems on the Manoa Campus and other off-site affiliated locations. This work involves lifting, carrying, pushing, or pulling equipment up to 50 pounds and may be performed under adverse conditions (e.g. confined spaces and inclement weather) and at elevated locations. *Installs, configures, maintains, patches, and troubleshoots to ensure that all departmental systems are running securely and data backups are completed as scheduled. *Works closely with UHM Information Technology Services (ITS) and Office of System Integration (OSI) to schedule, coordinate, and supervise the installation of network lines and other baseline IT systems within the DPS physical spaces. *Develops and designs test procedures to ensure functionality and security of systems. Evaluate, document, and recommend system adjustment or enhancement based on test results. Participates in the procurement and implementation of third-party software and hardware solutions by researching, evaluating and advising on appropriate hardware and software. *Provides instructions, technical training, and documentation to the public safety staff on the use of operational systems and field equipment. *Provides advanced technical assistance and troubleshooting when problems occur on operational equipment. *Works closely with external third-party vendors that provide specialized support services to maintain public safety equipment and resolve system discrepancies. *Works with ITS, and vendor to support and troubleshoot security systems and related devices that require monitoring throughout the UHM campus and other areas of responsibility. *Maintains the highest level of integrity and confidentiality in dealing with sensitive public safety data, system logs, and system feeds. *Follows and implements directives and guidance related to best practices from University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Services. *Ensures the consistent adoption, implementation, and enforcement of recommendations issued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service. *Keeps abreast of recommendations issued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service, and takes timely action as needed. *Continuously monitor and lead initiatives to enhance system reliability, security, and operational efficiency. *Attends off-site meetings, professional training programs, conferences, and external events to support organizational goals and stay aligned with operational requirements. *Must be available during non-standard working hours, including nights, weekends and holidays. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in arts, social science, arts and humanities, science, education, business or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for Multi-User System Administration or security systems and technical troubleshooting support, of which 2 years of the experience must have been comparable scope and complexity to the next lower pay band in the University of Hawaii System broadband system; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of security systems and technical troubleshooting support as demonstrated by the broad knowledge and understanding of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving information technology concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of the broad technology, systems, hardware and software associated with security systems and technical troubleshooting support. Demonstrated ability to recognize a wide range of intricate problems, use reasoning and logic to determine accurate causes, and apply principles and practices to determine, evaluate, integrate, and implement practical and thorough solutions in an effective and timely manner. Proven ability to comprehend, interpret and implement administrative directives and guidance to ensure IT operations align with organizational standards and industry best practices. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Ability to operate hydraulic lifts, climb ladders, and lift/carry tools and equipment needed to perform security system installation and maintenance. Ability to lift, carry, push and/or pull approximately up to 50 pounds at an elevated height, independently and/or with assistance. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work beyond normal hours, including evenings, weekends, and holidays as required. Working knowledge of monitoring and managing various types of security systems and devices such as CCTV, surveillance security cameras/DVR systems, fire alarms, card key access. Desirable Qualifications Direct professional experience working inside a public safety, law enforcement, emergency management, or government agency environment. Knowledge and experience in the utilization of the Genetec platform and/or Integrated Security Systems. Working knowledge of firewall configurations and implementation. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents by the closing date: Cover letter indicating how you meet the minimum qualifications; Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references (preferably supervisor); and Copy of your official transcript(s) showing up to highest degree. (Official transcripts will be required if selected to fill the position.) The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Note: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos, and PLEASE REDACT REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE ON TRANSCRIPT COPIES. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii Office of Campus Services, and will not be returned. Inquiries: Campus Services Human Resources Office; 808-956-6935; uhmcshr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529014/readvertisementcasual-hire-community-outreach-coordinator-ctahr-nrem": {
      "title": "***READVERTISEMENT***(Casual Hire) Community Outreach Coordinator (CTAHR/NREM)",
      "description": "***READVERTISEMENT***(Casual Hire) Community Outreach Coordinator (CTAHR/NREM) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02289 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience - NREM Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 10/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: CASUAL Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai’i may apply for Casual Hire appointments, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits provided to regular Board of Regents or Civil Service employees. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees.**** Title : ***READVERTISEMENT*** (Casual Hire) Community Outreach Coordinator (CTAHR/NREM) Position Number : Casual Hire Hiring Unit : College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resources (CTAHR), Natural Resources and Environmental Management (NREM) Location : University of Hawaii, Manoa, Hawaii Date Posted : October 26, 2026 Closing Date : October 30, 2026, 11:59 PM HST Salary : $27.00 - $30.00 per hour Full Time/Part Time : Part-time (up to 10 hours per week) Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : Appointment period from October 26, 2026 through March 31, 2027. This position is subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. For best consideration, all application materials should be submitted by October 21, 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before application review. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: Co-plan and execute a Nature-Based Solutions Exposition alongside the Principal Investigator (PI) and leadership team; direct on-site event operations, including registration, AV logistics, and post-event summaries. Oversee venue selection, catering, printing, and vendor contracts within budget constraints, ensuring full compliance with University of Hawai?i (UH) purchasing policies. Expedite end-to-end travel logistics, compiling travel requests, processing fiscal transactions, and completing post-travel reconciliations. Lead guest speaker coordination and community knowledge holder engagement; maintain master participant records and process honoraria distributions in compliance with institutional guidelines. Draft post-event reports, track participant engagement, and perform other key administrative duties as assigned. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business, Communications, Liberal arts and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience in N/A; or any equivalent combination of education/training or professional work experience. Functional knowledge of principles, practices, and techniques in event organization and/or administrative support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with event organization and/or administrative support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in event management and/or administrative support processes. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software. TO APPLY : Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Kirsten Oleson, koleson@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5524926/academic-advisor": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Academic Advisor Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02265 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - OVPSS - SAS - Student Athlete Academic Services Opening Date 10/21/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 97685 Description Title: Academic Advisor Position Number: 0097685 Hiring Unit: OFC OF VP FOR STUDENT SUCCESS, STUDENT ACADEMIC SUCCESS, MANOA ADVISORS Location: UH at Manoa Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Independently manage an assigned caseload of student-athletes and provide comprehensive academic advising regarding degree requirements, course selection, registration, academic policies and procedures, academic options, and progress toward degree. 2. *Interpret and apply University, college/school, major, NCAA, and conference academic requirements, policies, and procedures to individual student-athlete circumstances and provide appropriate guidance regarding academic progress and eligibility. 3. *Monitor, evaluate, and assess the academic progress of assigned student-athletes. Identify students who are or may be at- risk of experiencing academic difficulty and develop, coordinate, and monitor appropriate academic support and intervention strategies. 4. *Assist student-athletes in developing, evaluating, and modifying educational plans and academic and career goals consistent with their interests,abilities, degree requirements, and progress toward graduation. 5. *Assess student-athlete academic support needs and coordinate appropriate learning services, tutoring, mentoring, academic development, and other support resources. Make appropriate referrals to campus programs and services based on individual student needs. 6. *Review and analyze academic records to assess and document student-athlete progress toward degree and provide information and analysis necessary to support the determination of initial and continuing NCAA academic eligibility. Identify potential academic eligibility concerns and work collaboratively with appropriate University and Athletics personnel to address issues in a timely manner. 7. Conduct individual and group advising sessions,educational workshops, orientations, and informational sessions designed to promote academic success, persistence, graduation, and student development. 8. *Maintain accurate and comprehensive advising records and documentation of student academic progress, advising contacts, interventions, and support services.Prepare academic progress reports and other reports as required while maintaining appropriate confidentiality of student information and academic records. 9. Establish and maintain effective working relationships with faculty, academic departments, colleges, campus student-support programs, intercollegiate athletics personnel, and other University offices to facilitate student-athlete academic success and resolve complex academic issues. 10. Develop, review, and maintain advising resources, degree pathway documents, academic planning materials, and other tools used to assist student-athletes in understanding degree requirements and monitoring progress toward graduation. 11. *Maintain current knowledge of University academic policies, curricula, degree requirements, NCAA academic eligibility requirements,conference requirements, and evolving practices in academic advising and student success. Apply professional judgment and established best practices in providing advising and support services. 12. Participate in the development, implementation, and evaluation of advising initiatives, academic support programs, procedures, and services designed to enhance student-athlete academic success, retention, and graduation. 13. Work flexible hours, including evenings, weekends, and/or holidays, as required by athletic schedules, student needs, travel, and events. 14. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications Education and Professional Work Experience: 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Behavioral Sciences, Social Sciences, Humanities, Education, or related field, and three (3) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for providing academic advising for university-level students and/or learning support for university-level student-athletes; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills, and abilities as indicated. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: 2. Considerable working knowledge of the principles, practices, techniques in the area of academic advising and/or student support in higher education, as demonstrated by broad knowledge of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles, and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with academic advising and/or student support in higher education. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide-ranging, complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative, timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and other standard office productivity software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Ability to work respectfully and effectively with students who have diverse backgrounds and a wide range of academic abilities. 10. Demonstrated ability to use student information, academic advising, degree audit, and/or student tracking systems. 11. Demonstrated ability to exercise sound professional judgment and maintain confidentiality in handling sensitive student information and academic records. 12. Demonstrated ability to independently organize and manage multiple responsibilities, establish priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain accurate and detailed records. Desirable Qualifications 1. Master's degree from an accredited university in Behavioral Sciences, Social Sciences, Humanities, Education, or a related field. 2. Professional experience providing academic advising and/or learning support for university-level student-athletes. 3. Experience using Banner, STAR, Navigate360, ARMS/Teamworks, or comparable higher-education student information and advising systems. 4. Working knowledge of continuing NCAA academic eligibility rules. 5. Working knowledge of FERPA and University policies governing student academic records and confidentiality. To Apply: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries: Garrett Clanin at garrettc@hawaii.edu For best consideration, please submit your application by November 16, 2026. The first review of applications will begin after this date. EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5380035/administrative-officer-office-of-the-dean-0080187": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer (Office of the Dean) 0080187",
      "description": "Administrative Officer (Office of the Dean) 0080187 Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-01289 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene - Dean's Office Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 11/10/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0080187 Description Title: Administrative Officer (Office of the Dean) Position Number: 0080187 Hiring Unit: School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene (SONDH) Location: Honolulu / Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 26, 2026 (Readvertisement) Closing Date: November 10, 2026 Band: B Minimum Monthly Salary: Salary Schedules and Placement Information Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (100% FTE) Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions Position is expected to begin approximately December 2026 or as mutually acceptable. Renewal of appointment is dependent on performance, program needs and/or availability of funds. To know more about the SONDH, go to: https://nursing.hawaii.edu/ Duties and Responsibilities * Works closely with the Chief Administrative Officer (CAO) in the administration of the financial resources, services and systems of the SONDH, in collaboration with the Dean, Associate Dean(s), HSCN Director and heads of departments to ensure accurate & timely compliance with University policies and procedures, and state & federal rules & regulations. * Prepares, implements, analyzes and evaluates annual budget projections and expenditure plans in accordance with established instructions, guidelines and operating instructions. * Monitors, analyzes and interprets the department's financial condition and prepares financial reports for review of the CAO. * Certifies funding availability and propriety of expenditure on all documents initiated against organizational funds which include general, federal, private, revolving and other funding sources. * Signs or approves up to $15,000, or as designated by the CAO, subject to the approval of the Dean. * Initiates, processes, reviews, maintains, reconciles & audits fiscal transaction reports, requests, journals & records in accordance with federal, state, UH & UHF requirements, and identifies and resolves discrepancies and prepares adjustments/corrections. * Processes and monitors contracts and grants, including personnel, purchasing and disbursements, maintains accurate and timely records, and regularly produces reports on the financial status of various fund sources. * Provides staff support to principal investigators in the review, preparation, analysis, solicitation, evaluation, and negotiation of contracts and modifications of a moderately difficult and complex nature. * Serves as an alternate fiscal authority for SONDH; responsible for managing the day-to-day operations of SONDH's administrative offices in the absence of the CAO. * Perform administrative services for the department. * Prepares, processes & pre-audits fiscal-related docs like requisitions, purchase orders, payments, reimbursements, travel, inventory, purchasing card (PCard), interdepartmental orders & deposits for various funds to ensure appropriateness, propriety & compliance w/applicable laws, policies, procedures, rules & regulations. * Administers the PCard program of assigned units including setup and closure of purchasing card accounts by maintaining, managing, reconciling and auditing / correcting monthly purchasing card purchases. Keeps track and follows up with PCard holders regarding outstanding/delinquent Procurement Card Document (PCDO) in SAP Concur or supporting documents for PCDOs. * Serves as the primary department coordinator for the School's PCard Program with OPM, and point of contact for SONDH employees for questions regarding the School's PCard program. * Prepares internal and external correspondence, reports, budget justifications, and summaries including charts, tables and graphics to present information. * Reviews, develops and recommends administrative guidelines and procedures, and changes in the work systems for an effective and efficient delivery of service. * Provides technical advice, interpretations and/or makes effective recommendations on matters including but not limited to financial, purchasing and property management. * Resolves operational and administrative concerns/problems in accordance with applicable policies, procedures, rules and regulations. * Ensures all concerned offices and team members are updated on projects and policy changes by actively soliciting, receiving, and providing clear, accurate and timely feedback to improve team performance. * Works with HR in processing personnel/payroll-related transactions such as posting and/or approval of time sheets as the Business Office, generating payroll notification forms (UH/PNFs) as it relates to account changes or funding redistribution, among others, in accordance with UH and RCUH policies, procedures and deadlines. * Serves as the primary point of contact for RCUH-related personnel transactions for assigned departments. * Provides information and guidance to faculty/staff in the interpretation of UH personnel or administrative policies and procedures, bargaining unit contracts and other applicable State and Federal rules and regulations. * Serve as the School's designated building coordinator for facility related requests and activities. Works with CAO and HR in identifying best practices and/or issues/problems involving employees. Other Duties As Assigned. (*) Denotes Essential Duties. Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for financial management, cost accounting, contracts/grants management and/or personnel administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the financial management, cost accounting, contracts/grants management and/or personnel administration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with financial management, cost accounting, contracts/grants management and/or personnel administration. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Demonstrated ability to exercise judgment, tact, patience and discretion in applying and explaining policies and procedures. Demonstrated competence in customer service by providing helpful, courteous, accessible, responsive, and knowledgeable service to internal and external customers. Demonstrated ability in written and oral communications, exercising tact, diplomacy, and good judgment in relations with others. Demonstrated ability to analyze, interpret and apply fiscal, personnel and general administrative rules and regulations to a variety of work situations. Demonstrated ability in establishing and maintaining rapport with faculty, staff, federal administrators and the general public. Desirable Qualifications: Master's degree in Business Administration. Considerable knowledge of UH and RCUH policies and procedures. Familiarity with UH and/or RCUH Human Resources Information Systems. Knowledge of Kuali Financial System (KFS). Extensive experience with federal regulations pertaining to administering federal grants, contracts, and cooperative agreements and with applicable state regulations. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references; and Copies of unofficial transcripts (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries and copy of diplomas are NOT ACCEPTABLE. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. NOTE: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos they will be redacted. PLEASE REDACT Social Security #s and birth dates on transcript copies. All submitted items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. No late applications will be accepted. Inquiries: Corie O. Fulgencio-Arre, HR Specialist; mcfa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5520309/administrative-officer-senior-casual": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer, Senior (casual)",
      "description": "Administrative Officer, Senior (casual) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02232 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Medicine Opening Date 10/16/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: Casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai‘i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees (i.e. paid holidays/leave). Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full- time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Administrative Officer, Senior (casual) Position Number: Casual Hire Hiring Unit: C OF HLTH SCI & SW, SCH OF MED, MEDICINE DEPT Location: Department of Medicine, Queens Medical Center, 1356 Lusitana St, Honolulu, HI 96813, USA Date Posted: October 16, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment, first review October 23, 2026 Band: B Salary Range: $36.66/hour (minimum) Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary casual hire Other conditions: Casual Hire temporary appointment is expected begin November 2026, or soon thereafter. Duties and Responsibilities: *Serves as the Senior Administrative Officer for the Department of Medicine (DOM), John A. Burns School of Medicine (JABSOM) overseeing and managing the full spectrum of administrative, financial, operational, and personnel infrastructure functions. *Advises the Department Chair on strategic, financial, operational, and organizational matters affecting short and long-term departmental direction and goals. Financial decisions have direct impact on faculty compensation, staff payroll, research capacity, and the viability of educational, department and system programs. *Participates in the DOM strategic planning, developing and managing budgets while providing financial analysis and position oversight that drive organizational priorities across internal operations, external programs, and key community and health-system partnerships. *Produces authoritative, technically sound financial analyses and reports. Delivers forecasts and strategic recommendations that directly influence departmental policy, allocation of resources, and program development. *Manages comprehensive pre-award administration for complex federal and clinical research grants, collaborating closely with DOM Chair and faculty to spearhead budget formulation, ensure administrative compliance, and facilitate timely electronic submissions through myGRANT to the central Office of Research Services (ORS). Exercises seasoned diplomatic skill in managing and resolving personnel matters, fostering a culture of respect, accountability, and safety, maintaining institutional integrity. Manages professional associates and administrative support staff responsible for fiscal operations, HR processes, program administration, and departmental operations which include assigning, monitoring, evaluating, coaching and mentoring of staff. Ensures the DOM staff and faculty are trained in UH policies, fiscal procedures, compliance requirements, and operational systems. Develops and oversee budgets for extramural and endowment accounts ensuring compliance across all administrative domains. Develops new and improved fiscal and financial models, processes, reporting systems, which optimizes the tracking of key performance indicators (KPI) and internal controls to enhance transparency, operational efficiency, and strict donor and program compliance. Creates innovative financial and administrative operational workflows that support the Department’s three pillars in undergraduate and graduate medical education, research infrastructure development, and advancement of the clinical learning environments. *Designs and implements financial models, funding strategies, and resource allocation methods to advance and support program growth, faculty recruitment, and research expansion. Leads complex tasks requiring foresight, such as multi-year budget planning for complex department-wide operations, accreditation and cross-site systems and programs. Ensures effective stewardship of departmental resources, operational compliance, and strategic planning aligned with the mission of JABSOM and the DOM. Manages relationships with JABSOM leadership, health system partners, external agencies, and internal network of faculty. Coordinates communications with JABSOM and health system collaborators to resolve complex funding, payroll, human resources, and operational issues. Other duties as assigned. * denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, or Healthcare Administration, or related field and 6 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for administrative management leading and directing multi-functional teams involved in analyzing and reporting financials with key performance indicators, strategic planning, compensation models, academic medicine; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of financial management and planning as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with complex administrative functions, financial and operational systems, personnel administration, and academic medicine accreditation regulatory frameworks. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and [if applicable, add additional software]. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Management of multidisciplinary personnel performing complex duties at varied levels of expertise. Demonstrated knowledge and experience overseeing budget development, financial planning, procurement, contract administration, and operational policy implementation in a healthcare or academic environment. Strong record of building collaborative relationships with physicians, faculty, executive leadership, and external partners. Exceptional ability to produce high-impact executive reports including strategic reports, presentations, and decision-support materials. Experience developing and revising policies and workflows. Strong knowledge of risk management, internal controls, records management, and organizational compliance frameworks. Demonstrated commitment to ethical governance, regulatory compliance, and consistent application of organizational policies. Exercises seasoned diplomatic skill in managing and resolving personnel matters, fostering a culture of respect, accountability, and safety, maintaining institutional integrity. Desirable Qualifications Possession of a Master’s degree in business administration or related field. 10+ years of management at director-level administrative leadership, including oversight of department wide operations, strategic planning, and organizational transformation initiatives. Proven track record managing complex organizational operations, workflow redesign, and management of large, cross-organizational teams with staff operating at different technical and administrative skill tiers. At least 5 years of experience managing budgets exceeding $3M , including forecasting, financial modeling, and long-range resource planning. Knowledge of accreditation councils and committee guidelines; i.e. Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME), Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME), Accreditation Council for Continuing Medical Education (ACCME). Working knowledge of the University of Hawaii financial systems, UH Foundation, and/or Research Corporation of the University of Hawaii (RCUH) online systems. Proven understanding and experience working with complex government, state and University funds including General, Special, extramural, and revolving account funds. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, Current resume Official transcripts confirming degree completion (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree granting institution required upon hire.) Three (3) professional references (complete with contact information including e-mail address) The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Address: Department of Medicine c/o Human resources 1356 Lusitania Street 7th Floor Honolulu, HI 96813 Inquiries: Scott K. Kuwada, MD – skkuwada@hawaii.edu Maria Inong – minong@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5526671/assistant-or-associate-professor-gme-preceptor-neighbor-island-0084159c": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (GME Preceptor Neighbor Island, 0084159C)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor (GME Preceptor Neighbor Island, 0084159C) Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Various Locations - Hawai'i Island, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02275 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Pediatrics Opening Date 10/22/2026 Closing Date 11/6/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0084159C Description Title: Assistant or Associate Professor (H3/H4M11) Position Number: 0084159C Hiring Unit: John A. Burns School of Medicine – Pediatrics Location: Affiliated Clinical Site(s) on Hawai`i Island or other neighbor island Date Posted: October 22, 2026 Closing Date: November 6, 2026 Salary Information: Salary commensurate with qualifications and experience. Monthly Type: 11 Month Tenure Track: Non-Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Part Time - Full Time Equivalency: 25% Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Renewable subject to performance and/or funding availability;to begin approximately November 2026 subject to position clearance and availability of funds. Duties and Responsibilities: For the Assistant Professor: Participates in the clinical precepting and assessment of residents and other learners. Actively participates in Department of Pediatrics or school faculty development activities to promote their growth as a medical educator and role model for medical learners. Maintains educational liaison with the local hospital or health system as it relates to clinical rotation requirements. Actively participates in the Department of Pediatrics GME program educational and administrative activities, including, but not limited to academic half-day sessions (e.g., provide periodic lectures or workshops), program evaluation committee meetings, and department or JABSOM-related community medicine or population health activities. In collaboration with the JABSOM GME Program Director and department Clerkship Director for the clinical subspecialty, and the JABSOM Office of Medical Education, assures the quality of the clinical and educational experience for the residents and medical students by ensuring that the requirements established by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education, Liaison Committee on Medical Education, and applicable specialty board are met and maintained in the clinical site. May serve in a formal educational administrative role, as determined by the clinical department chair or JABSOM Serves on JABSOM, University committees and/or the Medical Executive Committee of the affiliated site, as appropriate. Performs research or comparable scholarly activity. Performs related tasks as assigned. Duties and Responsibilities For the Associate Professor: Same as Assistant Professor but with increasing responsibility for administering programs in research, education and/or clinical care; and with less supervision and oversight. Minimum Qualifications: M.D./D.O. degree from an LCME/AOA (Liaison Committee on Medical Education/American Osteopathic Association) accredited medical school. American Board of Pediatrics Board certification (within 7 years). Eligible for licensure in Hawaii. Demonstrated ability in teaching and patient care. Poise and good address for meeting and conferring with others. Eligible for an appointment in good standing with the local hospital and/or health system on island. Associate Professor Minimum Qualifications In addition to the minimum qualifications, the Associate Professor position must have-- a minimum of four years in the rank of Assistant Professor (candidates not applying from the assistant professor rank should have seven or more years of post-doctoral experience in a recognized medical school or major medical research/scholarship institution) or one year at the rank of Associate Professor at another medical school of recognized standing. Proven ability in teaching, research, or program administration. Evidence of significant scholarly contribution to the clinical specialty. Ability to serve as a role model for students and junior colleagues. Specialty board certification and recertification in that field when appropriate. Desirable Qualifications: A minimum of two years of full-time professional activity in the clinical specialty and have had teaching experience in a medical school or residency program. Prior experience with networking and advocacy for improved health of rural or underserved population. To apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, Curriculum Vitae Names and contact information of five professional references, Copy of U.S. Board Certification AND current U.S. medical license or official transcripts from medical school plus certificates of completion from residency training and current U.S. medical license. Copies of transcripts are accepted when used instead of current U.S. board certification or current U.S. medical license. However, if used for verification, original transcripts are required upon hire. The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Inquiries: Tumaini Rucker Coker, M.D., M.B.A., Department Chair; 808-369-1237 or tumaini.coker@hphmg.org EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5520111/assistant-professor-developmental-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Developmental Psychology)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Developmental Psychology) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02229 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Social Sciences - Psychology Opening Date 10/19/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: pending Description Title: Assistant Professor (Developmental Psychology) Position Number: Pending Position Number Hiring Unit: UHM College of Social Sciences - Psychology Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 19, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous; application review begins on November 19, 2026. Applications received by November 18, 2026, will be given priority. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/ Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: The Department of Psychology, College of Social Sciences (CSS), University of Hawai`i at Manoa (UHM, Honolulu, Hawai’i) invites applicants for the position of Assistant Professor (F-3) who will add to the department’s strengths in Developmental Psychology in our Community, Cultural, and Developmental (CCD) program. This is a 9-month, full-time, permanent, tenure-track faculty appointment to begin August 1, 2027, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. More information about the department is available at https://psychology.manoa.hawaii.edu/ About the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa: UHM is an R1 (Very High Research Activity, Carnegie Classification) institution located on a beautiful arboretum campus. UHM is a land- grant, sea-grant, and space-grant university with the core mission of generating, preserving, and disseminating knowledge for the greater good of the people of the state of Hawai‘i and the world beyond through teaching, research, and service. We seek a dynamic and dedicated individual who is passionate about research, teaching, and contributing to the academic community as well as demonstrates commitment to, knowledge of, and/or experience with supporting the mission and purpose of UH as a leading indigenous-serving higher education institution. Duties and Responsibilities : Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in developmental psychology, including a required graduate course in Cognitive Development. Supervise and mentor undergraduates, MA and PhD students. Establish an active scholarly research program; undertake research/scholarly projects in the field or branch of learning represented by and consistent with the goals of the UHM CSS. Disseminate research findings at professional conferences and through publications. Seek extramural funding. Provide service to the department, college, university, and community. Maintain a professional level of performance and productivity in assigned areas of teaching, research, and service. Other duties as assigned by the Department Chair. Minimum Qualifications : Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely related field from an accredited college or university or foreign equivalent, with a research program explicitly grounded in developmental psychology. ABDs will be considered but all degree requirements must be completed and degree conferred prior to date of hire; Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in developmental psychology, including a graduate course in cognitive development; Ability to mentor students in area of expertise. Ability to conduct empirical work and demonstrate how it directly tests, extends, or contributes to specific developmental psychology theory or theories (e.g., cognitive, socio-emotional, ecological, cultural, or lifespan development theories); Record of research achievement in Developmental Psychology commensurate with time in field; Demonstrated ability to maintain professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Desirable Qualifications : Ability to foster collaborative, interdisciplinary dialogue with colleagues working in cultural and community contexts. Experience teaching student populations with varying backgrounds. Demonstrated ability to secure external funding. To Apply: Please submit the following required documents at https:// academicjobsonline.org/ajo/ jobs/33122 : Cover letter that addresses the minimum and desirable qualifications Curriculum vitae A statement of research that explicitly identifies the specific developmental psychology theory or theories the work builds upon, and detail how the findings contribute to or advance those theoretical frameworks A statement of teaching experience and interests Summaries of teaching evaluations Up to three (3) representative publications Names and contact information of 3 professional references (recommendation letters to be submitted for short-list candidates); Official transcripts (copies are acceptable, however, official transcripts will be required upon hire) . Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Inquiries: Dr. Yiyuan Xu, Search Committee Co-Chair, yiyuan@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5520037/assistant-professor-economics-in-innovation-and-technology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Economics in Innovation and Technology)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Economics in Innovation and Technology) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02227 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Social Sciences - Economics Opening Date 10/22/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: pending Description Title: Assistant Professor (Economics of Innovation and Technology) Position Number: Pending Position Number Hiring Unit: UHM College of Social Sciences – Department of Economics Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: 10/22/2026 Closing Date: Continuous; review of applications will begin 12/01/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received by 11/30/2026 will be given priority. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: The Department of Economics, College of Social Sciences, University of Hawai'i at Manoa (UHM, Honolulu, Hawai'i) invites applicants for the position of Assistant Professor (F-3) in the economics of innovation and technology. This is a 9-month, full-time, permanent, tenure-track faculty appointment to begin August 1, 2027, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. We are especially interested in candidates whose research examines the economic consequences of artificial intelligence (AI) and related technologies, including effects on industry structure, labor demand, employment, skills, and wages; productivity and economic growth; inequality and the distribution of economic gains; environment and natural resources; healthcare delivery, access, and health outcomes; education, and other dimensions of well-being. We welcome applicants from all fields of economics whose research is broadly related to these themes. Relevant fields include, but are not limited to, labor economics, macroeconomics and growth, industrial organization, public economics, development economics, international trade, health and education economics, behavioral economics, econometrics, and computational economics, as well as the broader economics of innovation, entrepreneurship, and technological change. Applicants need not identify AI or innovation as their primary field, provided their research agenda engages meaningfully with these questions. More information about the Department of Economics is available at https://www.economics.hawaii.edu . This position will contribute to UHM's broader interdisciplinary efforts in AI research and education. Opportunities exist for collaboration with Information & Computer Sciences, Electrical & Computer Engineering, and other units across campus, including on externally funded research. UHM is an R1 (Very High Research Activity, Carnegie Classification) institution located on a beautiful arboretum campus. UHM is a land-grant, sea-grant, space-grant, and sun-grant university with the core mission of generating, preserving, and disseminating knowledge for the greater good of the people of the state of Hawai'i and the world beyond. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses such as Principles of Economics, Economics of Artificial Intelligence, Economics of Innovation and Technological Change, and courses in the applicant's field of expertise, which may include quantitative and data-analytic methods offered as part of the College's SODA (Social Data Analytics) initiative; Supervise and mentor MA and PhD students; Publish scholarly, peer-reviewed research in areas of expertise; Seek extramural funding in areas of expertise; Provide service to the department, university, and community; Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Economics or a related field from a college or university of recognized standing, or foreign equivalent. ABDs will be considered, but all degree requirements must be completed and a PhD conferred prior to the date of hire; Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the area of expertise; Ability to mentor and advise students in the applicant's area of research; A record of research achievement (publications, grants) commensurate with time in field; Demonstrated ability to maintain professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Desirable Qualifications: Research that addresses the economic effects of artificial intelligence or other technological change on labor markets, productivity, growth, inequality, or well-being; Experience using AI, machine learning, or other computational and data-intensive methods in economic research; Demonstrated ability to secure extramural research funding, including through interdisciplinary collaboration; Ability to develop new courses on the economics of AI and technological change, including courses for a general or non-major audience. To Apply: JEL Classifications: 00 Default: Any Field C Mathematical and Quantitative Methods D Microeconomics E Macroeconomics and Monetary Economics F International Economics H Public Economics I Health, Education, and Welfare J Labor and Demographic Economics L Industrial Organization O Economic Development, Innovation, Technological Change, and Growth Q Agricultural and Natural Resource Economics • Environmental and Ecological Economics Keywords: Artificial Intelligence; Technological Change; Innovation; Automation; Machine Learning Please submit the following required documents at https://www.aeaweb.org/joe/ listing.php?JOE_ID=2026-02_ 111478370 . Cover letter addressing the minimum and desirable qualifications; Curriculum vitae; Statement of research experience and interests; Statement of teaching experience and interests; Summaries of teaching evaluations (if available); A job market paper and up to two additional working papers; Three (3) letters of recommendation; Official transcripts (copies are acceptable for application; official transcripts will be required upon hire). Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Inquiries Michael J. Roberts , Search Committee Chair, mjrobert@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5520081/assistant-professor-social-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Social Psychology)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Social Psychology) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02228 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Social Sciences - Psychology Opening Date 10/20/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: pending Description Title: Assistant Professor (Social Psychology) Position Number: Pending Position Number Hiring Unit: UHM College of Social Sciences - Psychology Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 20, 2026 Closing Date : Continuous; application review begins on November 21, 2026. Applications received by November 20, 2026 will be given priority. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/ Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: The Department of Psychology, College of Social Sciences (CSS), University of Hawai’i at Manoa (UHM, Honolulu, Hawai’i) invites applicants for the position of Assistant Professor (F-3) position in Social Psychology. The hire will join the Cognitive, Neuroscience, and Social area, and desired applicants will add to the department’s strengths in cognition, neuroscience, and/or social psychology and have expertise in psychological intervention and social influence, although any area of social psychology will be considered. This is a 9-month, full-time, permanent, tenure-track faculty appointment to begin August 1, 2027, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. More information about the department is available at https://psychology.manoa.hawaii.edu/ About the University of Hawai‘i at Manoa: UHM is an R1 (Very High Research Activity, Carnegie Classification) institution located on a beautiful arboretum campus. UHM is a land- grant, sea-grant, and space-grant university with the core mission of generating, preserving, and disseminating knowledge for the greater good of the people of the state of Hawai‘i and the world beyond through teaching, research, and service. Duties and Responsibilities: Develop a research program; undertake research/scholarly projects in the field or branch of learning represented by and consistent with the goals of the UHM Department of Psychology and the Cognition, Neuroscience, and Social Area . Seek extramural funding. Publish research in peer-reviewed journals. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in social psychology, seminars in specialty area, and courses serving departmental needs (e.g., Statistical Techniques in Psychology). Supervise and mentor undergraduate, MA and PhD students. Maintain a professional level of performance and productivity in assigned areas of teaching, research, and service. Contribute to development activities at the discipline, department, and campus levels. Work independently and with colleagues to select, develop, and revise curricular materials, instructional techniques, and assessment strategies at the course, credential and/or discipline levels. Provide service to department, college, university, and community. Participate in professional and self-development activities. Other duties as assigned by the Department Chair. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Psychology with an emphasis in Social Psychology, or closely related area from an accredited college or university or foreign equivalent. ABDs will be considered, but all degree requirements must be completed and a PhD conferred prior to the date of hire; Record of research accomplishment (e.g., publications, grants) commensurate with time in the field; Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in social psychology and seminars in specialty area; Demonstrated ability to mentor students in the applicant’s area of research; Demonstrated ability to maintain professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Desirable Qualifications : Post-doctoral or comparable research experience. Demonstrated ability to secure extramural funding. Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate and/or graduate statistics, which could include stats courses relevant to applied psychology (e.g., SEM, MLM, longitudinal data analysis, statistics for program evaluation). Experience teaching student populations with varying backgrounds. To Apply: Please submit the following required documents at https:// academicjobsonline.org/ajo/ jobs/33169 : Cover letter addressing how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Curriculum vitae; A statement summarizing your program of research; A statement summarizing your teaching philosophy and interests; Three (3) relevant publications; Summaries of teaching evaluations; Contact information for three (3) recommenders (references will be contacted upon being shortlisted). Finalists must be prepared to submit copies of official transcripts prior to campus visit. Official transcripts will be required upon hire. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Inquiries: Dr. Joni Sasaki, Search Committee Chair, 808-956-6673, yjoni@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5523546/assistant-professor-southeast-asian-history-pos-0082164": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Southeast Asian History) (pos. 0082164)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Southeast Asian History) (pos. 0082164) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02256 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - CALL - Department of History Opening Date 10/20/2026 Position Number: 0082164 Description Title: Assistant Professor (Southeast Asian History) Position Number: 00 82164 Hiring Unit: College of Arts, Languages & Letters Location: Department of History Date Posted: 10/20 /2026 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins 11/21/2026 Salary Information: Salary commensurate with qualifications and experience. Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Funding: General Funds The Department of History invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor in the history of Southeast Asia to begin August 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funds. The area and period of research in Southeast Asian history are open. Candidates specializing in the early modern period, Mainland Southeast Asia, or Islamic Southeast Asia are especially encouraged to apply. We also welcome applications from scholars whose research transcends conventional approaches to Southeast Asian history, including transnational or transregional scholarship on themes such as (im)mobility, the environmental and digital humanities, comparative colonialism, and religion. The successful candidate will be expected to publish peer reviewed research and teach Southeast Asia survey courses, as well as general and advanced undergraduate and graduate courses in their areas of expertise. Southeast Asian History and Southeast Asian Studies at UHM are leading programs in the United States and the world. Its reputable faculty have produced groundbreaking scholarship and graduates who are now academics in prominent universities and successful individuals in the public and private sectors. Because of the strength of this program, UHM is consistently a top destination for students and scholars in Southeast Asian Studies, offering many Southeast Asian languages and financial support to hundreds of students. The new faculty member in Southeast Asian history is expected to facilitate interdisciplinary dialogue and transregional collaboration with other academic departments and area studies centers. Moreover, the successful candidate will help strengthen UHM’s ties with Southeast Asian communities in Hawai‘i, meet the evolving demands of the growing Southeast Asian student body, and enrich historical understandings of the greater Asia-Pacific region. Our campus welcomes all students but given our responsibilities to the community in which we operate, as part of our strategic mission, we strive to become a Native Hawaiian Place of Learning, a place that is committed to integrating Native Hawaiian language, culture and/or values into its academic and campus environment. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach lower-division undergraduate survey courses in Southeast Asian history; Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in area(s) of specialization and in general areas based on the needs of the department. ; Supervise MA and PhD students; Participate in graduate committees; Conduct research and publish in areas of specialization; Perform department, college and university service work; Other duties as assigned by the department chair. Minimum Qualifications: PhD or equivalent degree in History, or a related discipline (e.g., Asian Studies, Anthropology, Religion) from a college or university of recognized standing (ABD will be considered; all requirements for degree must be completed by August 2027); Demonstrated expertise in Southeast Asian studies or interdisciplinary Asian studies as evidenced by scholarly research and publications; Evidence of relevant teaching experience or demonstrated ability or potential to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels; Research proficiency in at least one Southeast Asian language; Evidence of strong research capabilities; Demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially with students, faculty, staff and administrators. Desirable Qualifications: Specialization in the early modern period in Southeast Asian history, mainland Southeast Asia or Islamic Southeast Asia; Demonstrated ability to teach other history survey courses such as World History; Ability to work in or understand at least two languages other than English. Demonstrated excellence in teaching, training, and supervision of graduate and/or undergraduate students. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Current CV; Academic transcripts showing highest degree received (copies are acceptable but official transcripts required at the time of hire); List of professional references from whom letters of recommendation will be requested by the applicant. Three confidential letters of recommendation - upload link (name file: Applicant Name-Reference Letter): https://www.dropbox.com/request/xc9iovirh5u6ehjfldvh . Please let your references know to submit the letters directly; invitations will not be sent. Additional materials (e.g. teaching samples and syllabi, writing sample, published work, etc.) may be requested at a later date. Inquiries: Dr. Vina Lanzona, Chair of the Search Committee, vlanzona@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5525109/assistant-professor-learning-design-technology-0085201": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Learning Design & Technology (0085201)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Learning Design & Technology (0085201) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02269 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Division Learning Design and Technology Opening Date 10/21/2026 Position Number: 0085201 Description Title: Assistant Professor - Learning Design & Technology (0085201) Position Number: 0085201 Hiring Unit: College of Education, Department of Learning Design & Technology (LTEC) Location: Manoa Date Posted: October 21, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous – Review of applications begins November 16, 2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: General funds, contingent upon position clearance. Appointment to begin August 2027 or soon thereafter. Selectee is required to complete the COE New Faculty Orientation in the first year, successfully pass a criminal background check, fulfill all mandatory training and compliance requirements and maintain an on-campus presence. Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate and graduate courses, including doctoral level research courses in face-to-face, online and/or hybrid formats, as determined by departmental needs. Advise graduate students in traditional and distance education modes. Hold regular in-person office hours to support student learning and engagement. Attend in-person departmental and college meetings. Maintain an active program of research and publication. Participate in accreditation processes, including documentation and report preparation. Provide service to the department, college, university, and community. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications An earned doctorate in Learning Design and Technology, Educational Technology or closely related field from a college or university of recognized standing; ABD candidates will be considered if the degree is conferred by date of hire. Evidence of research productivity. Evidence of successful face-to-face, online and hybrid teaching. Evidence of expertise with innovative and emerging technologies (e.g., Artificial Intelligence in Educational Practice, Learning Analytics, Extended Reality, Computer Science in Education) with an emphasis on applying these tools to build student workforce skills and tech literacy. Coursework in or expertise with multiple research methodologies. Evidence of strong interpersonal/collaboration skills. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively through diverse media. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience that provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Desirable Qualifications Demonstrated ability to initiate new research and programs that will enhance the department and college. Record of securing external funding for research and/or program implementation. Demonstrated commitment to and expertise in place-based pedagogy. 1 year of experience teaching instructional design principles. 1 year of experience with program assessment and accreditation, including knowledge of AECT, CSTA, ISTE-ASCD (formerly ISTE) and other relevant standards; 1 year of experience with graduate student mentorship and advising with particular emphasis on research and dissertations; 1 year of experience working in, or collaborating with, K-12 environments. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2. Curriculum vitae 3. Names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 5 professional references, 4. Official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire) 5. 1-3 reprints of scholarly publications. Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Peter Leong/Kitty Hino; 808-956-7671; peterleo@hawaii.edu / hinok@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5525106/assistant-professor-school-counseling-0085187-0082827": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085187 & 0082827)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085187 & 0082827) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02268 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Division Educational Psychology Opening Date 10/21/2026 Position Number: 0085184 & 0082827 Description Title: Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085187 & 0082827) Position Number: 0085184 & 0082827-2 positions Hiring Unit: College of Education, Department of Educational Psychology (EDEP) Location: Manoa Date Posted: October 21, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins December 2, 2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Track Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: General funds, contingent upon position clearance. Appointment to begin August 2027 or soon thereafter. Selectee is required to complete the COE New Faculty Orientation in the first year, successfully pass a criminal background check, fulfill all mandatory training and compliance requirements and maintain an on-campus presence. One of the selectees may be appointed as the Director of School Counseling, an 11-month position. The Director appointment is for one-year and may be renewed annually. The Director is responsible for program oversight, administration, accreditation, and strategic growth. The second position will coordinate and support the practicum and internship components of the program. Duties and Responsibilities Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate classes in School Counseling and Educational Psychology in face-to-face, online and/or hybrid formats, as determined by departmental needs. Plan and implement the School Counseling program, including developing course syllabi, course scheduling, and program assessments. Coordinate field practicum and internship placements, preparing and supervising field practicum supervisors. Plan and implement program recruitment, admission and retention efforts. Advise and mentor School Counseling students. Hold regular in-person office hours to support student learning and engagement. Maintain alignment with State and national accrediting agencies and complete accreditation processes. Participate in accreditation processes, including documentation and report preparation. Coordinate with the Office of Student Academic Services (OSAS) for submission of students’ completion data. Establish and maintain effective work relationships with staff and colleagues. Facilitate partnerships with local schools and school counseling organizations. Utilize excellent oral and written communication and presentation skills. Utilize excellent strategic and analytic thinking skills to solve problems and make decisions. Attend in-person departmental and college meetings. Maintain an active program of research/scholarly activity and publications in area of specialization. Perform service to the department, college, university and community. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications An earned doctorate in Counselor Education, Mental Health Counseling, Educational Psychology, Psychology or closely related field from a college or university of recognized standing; ABD candidates will be considered if the degree is conferred by date of hire. 1 year of experience as a school or mental health counselor. Expertise working with students from varied populations. Evidence of research/scholarly productivity in school counseling or a related area. Desirable Qualifications Holds licensure as a school counselor. 1 year of experience teaching graduate students in school counseling or a related field. 1 year of experience mentoring and supervising students in school counseling field settings. Evidence of successful teaching across a range of school counseling courses. Demonstrated history of successful leadership outcomes in higher education or other educational institutions. Evidence of successful teaching of online or hybrid courses in higher education. Strong working knowledge and understanding of established professional code of ethics and best practices. Understanding of current issues facing school counseling in Hawai‘i. Evidence of partnerships with school and community organizations. Evidence of scholarly activities related to counseling. Publications and grant funding related to research agenda. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2. Curriculum vitae 3. Names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references 4. Official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire) 5. Summaries of student evaluations of higher education courses taught previously 6. 1-3 reprints of selected scholarly publications Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Lois Yamauchi; yamauchi@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5526652/assistant-professor-associate-professor-mathematics-0083172-0082036": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor Mathematics (0083172 / 0082036)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor Mathematics (0083172 / 0082036) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02274 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - Mathematics Opening Date 10/22/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 00831712 / 0082036 Description Title: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor Position Number: 0083172 / 0082036 Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences – Department of Mathematics Location: University of Hawai’i at Manoa Date Posted: 10/22/2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment until position is filled – application review begins November 30, 2026 Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track:Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Mathematics Department at the University of Hawai’i at Manoa (UHM) invites applications for two permanent, full-time (9-month) positions, one at the Assistant Professor level and one at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, to begin August 1 st , 2027, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. Special consideration will be given to applicants whose research aligns with departmental needs, including geometry/topology, probability/statistics and applied mathematics/modeling. However, applicants from all areas of mathematics are encouraged to apply. Duties and Responsibilities 1. Conduct assigned courses or seminars 2. Conduct research or undertake comparable scholarly activity 3. Serve on departmental committees 4. Perform other tasks and/or duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications 1. Ph.D. in mathematics or related field from a college or university of recognized standing 2. Demonstrated research and scholarly achievement 3. Demonstrated ability as a teacher 4. Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others Desirable Qualifications Research agenda that synergizes with existing research in the department To Apply: Electronic application at www.mathjobs.org is preferred. Alternately, applicants may click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete their application and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit the following: A cover letter A curriculum vitae A list of publications A statement of research done and planned A statement of teaching philosophy and must arrange for At least three confidential letters of recommendation, at least one of which must address teaching, to be sent by email, or regular mail at the address below. In their cover letter, applicants should indicate whether they wish to be considered at the Assistant Professor or Associate professor level, or both, and are encouraged to mention any connections with existing faculty members’ research interests. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. All complete applications received prior to November 30, 2026 will receive full consideration; we will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. Official transcripts with degree conferred will be required upon hire. Address: University of Hawai’i at Manoa Department of Mathematics 2565 McCarthy Mall, Keller 401A Honolulu HI 96822 Inquiries : Erik Guentner, guentner@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529168/assistant-associate-professor-h3-h4m11-84317t": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor (H3/H4M11, 84317T)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor (H3/H4M11, 84317T) Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02288 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Surgery Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 11/10/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0084317T Description Title: Assistant/Associate Professor; H3-H4M11 Position Number: 0084317T Hiring Unit: John A. Burns School of Medicine, Department of Surgery Location: Queen’s University Tower Campus Date Posted: October 26, 2026 Closing Date: November 10,2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with education and experience Monthly Type: 11 months Tenure Track: Non-tenure Full Time/Part Time: Part-time, 15% FTE Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Funding: F Other conditions: To begin approximately December 2026, subject to position clearance. Non-tenure track; 11-month appointment period; annually renewable depending on performance and/or funding availability Duties and Responsibilities: Serve as the Department of Surgery’s champion for neighbor island medical education expansion and a key liaison between JABSOM and neighbor island hospitals, community clinics, and surgical practices. Participate in and help facilitate faculty development activities with the relevant JABSOM faculty professional development leads that strengthen clinical teaching, learner assessment, mentorship, and support for community-based surgical educators. Assist the Department of Surgery leadership in developing and implementing policies, affiliation agreements, and initiatives that support the expansion of surgical education across the neighbor islands. Assess surgical training capacity within the context of LCME (clerkship) and ACGME specialty requirements at existing and prospective neighbor island sites, including inpatient, outpatient, and operative experiences, and work with JABSOM and local partners to address barriers to establishing and sustaining rotations. Build and maintain relationships with neighbor island surgeons and other healthcare professionals to expand participation as clinical preceptors and faculty, and assist with their recruitment, onboarding, orientation, and ongoing support. Collaborate with healthcare leaders and surgeons on Hawai`i Island, Maui, Kaua ' i, Moloka ' i, and Lana ' i to identify, develop, and sustain high-quality clinical rotations for third- and fourth-year medical students and JABSOM surgical residents, consistent with local resources and community needs. With the clerkship director and/or residency program director, develop educational experiences that expose medical students and residents to rural surgical practice, challenges in accessing surgical care, and the role of community-based surgeons in meeting local healthcare needs. Participate in the Department of Surgery and JABSOM rural health, community medicine, or population health initiatives that advance surgical education and support the development of Hawaii’s physician workforce. Work closely with the JABSOM Medical Student Rural Education Director, Surgery Clerkship Director, Surgery Residency Program Director, and Office of Medical Education to guide student and resident placements, align learning experiences with curricular objectives, and promote continuity across training sites. Collaborate with relevant JABSOM educational and program leaders to monitor the quality of clinical learning experiences and support compliance with applicable Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME), Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME), and specialty board requirements. Travel periodically to conduct neighbor-island or rural O ' ahu site visits to assess learning environments, obtain feedback from learners and preceptors, strengthen partnerships, guide improvements in surgical education, and participate in faculty professional development. Assistant Professor Minimum Qualifications M.D./D.O. degree from an accredited medical school. Current American Board of Surgery Certification/Participates in Continuous Certification or equivalent. Eligible for or currently licensed to practice in Hawai 'i. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills. Excellent organizational skills. Evidence of skill in small group teaching, delivering lectures, and participating in conferences and colloquia, as well as expertise as a resource person. Ability to serve as a role model for learners and junior colleagues. Able to travel to the neighbor islands in Hawai 'i. Desirable Qualifications Demonstrated experience, including scholarship, in developing and delivering faculty development programs for clinician educators, including teaching, assessment, feedback, coaching, supervision, curriculum design, or educational quality improvement. Demonstrated ability to work and teach collaboratively with other faculty within and across disciplines. Comfortable working in a multi-cultural setting. Associate Professor Minimum Qualifications M.D./D.O. degree from an accredited medical school. Current American Board of Surgery Certification/Participates in Continuous Certification or equivalent. Eligible for or currently licensed to practice in Hawai 'i. A minimum of four (4) years in the rank of Assistant Professor or four years of other medical experience, or a total of four (4) years of teaching in the rank of Assistant Professor and other medical experience, or one (1) year at the rank of Associate Professor at another accredited medical school. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills. Excellent organizational skills. Evidence of skill in small group teaching, delivering lectures, and participating in conferences and colloquia, as well as expertise as a resource person. Experience with teaching methods that emphasize active, self-directed learning by learners. Ability to serve as a role model for learners and junior colleagues. Evidence of research/scholarship in medical education or related areas. Able to travel to the neighbor islands in Hawai 'i. Desirable Qualifications Advanced degree in medical education, academic medicine or a similar field. Demonstrated experience, including scholarship, in developing and delivering faculty development programs for clinician educators, including teaching, assessment, feedback, coaching, supervision, curriculum design, or educational quality improvement. Demonstrated ability to work and teach collaboratively with other faculty within and across disciplines. Comfortable working in a multi-cultural setting. Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following REQUIRED documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications CV Names and contact information of five professional references Evidence of current U.S. Board Certification and continuation/maintenance of certification (printout from an ABMS board website is acceptable) The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Note : If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Maria Chun, PhD mariachu@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5496536/casual-hire-administrative-fiscal-support-office-of-the-dean": {
      "title": "CASUAL HIRE - Administrative & Fiscal Support (Office of the Dean)",
      "description": "CASUAL HIRE - Administrative & Fiscal Support (Office of the Dean) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02056 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene - Dean's Office Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 10/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: CASUAL Description ***** Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular Board of Regents (BOR) and Civil Service employees (i.e. paid holidays/leave). Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Administrative and Fiscal Support (Office of the Dean) Position Number: ***** CASUAL HIRE (89 Days) Hiring Unit: School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene Location: Manoa Campus / Honolulu Date Posted: October 26, 2026 Closing Date: October 30, 2026 Salary Range: C ommensurate with qualifications and experience. Full Time/Part Time: Full/Part-Time, 50% to 100% (not to exceed 89 days; variable work hour schedule) Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions Position is expected to begin in November 2026 or as mutually acceptable; not to exceed 89 days. Duties and Responsibilities Create and manage project activities and project management documents, i.e., action plans, timelines, process flow maps, etc. Independently create and draft substantive letters, reports, and documents for the Dean’s and Associate Deans' Offices. Conduct literature searches, web searches, and determines relevancy of and summarizes information into summary reports for review by leadership. Provide logistical support for event planning and coordination, including communicating and coordinating with vendors as assigned, preparing event materials and supplies, and assisting with event-related administrative tasks. Assists with event preparation, set-up, implementation, and breakdown, including organizing materials and equipment. Demonstrate the highest standards of customer service and responds appropriately to inquiries and concerns demonstrating high level problem-solving and communication skills. Provide fiscal support for purchasing and travel. Serve as back-up for other institutional support personnel as needed. Coordinate meetings and record meeting minutes or summaries as requested. Other duties as requested. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's in business administration, social sciences, communications, education, evaluation or related field with 3 years of experience in project management; Self-starter with demonstrated ability to learn new operating systems quickly; Demonstrated ability to be well-organized, multi-task, pay attention to detail, and effectively manage projects; Demonstrated ability to effectively participate and collaborate as a member of a team; Demonstrates a high level of knowledge and skill in customer service: responsive, proactive, and calm in crisis situations; Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Office Professional Suite or related programs; Ability to write technically sound and descriptive reports and procedures, exercise proper grammar skills, and make concise presentations to targeted audiences; Strong written and verbal communication skills; Ability to problem solve complex problems with tact and diplomacy and referring issues as appropriate to others. Desirable Qualifications Experience in support services in an academic environment. Previous work experience with University of Hawai'i Administrative Policies and Procedures. Familiarity with course overviews and/or course evaluation processes. To Apply Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and upload the required documents. Required documents include: Cover letter indicating interest in the position and how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Current resume / curriculum vitae; Names and contact information (including email address) of at least three (3) professional references; Copies of official transcripts (original official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries and copy of diplomas are NOT ACCEPTABLE. Redact Social Security #s and birth dates on transcript copies. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Please ensure all required documents are attached before submission, as incomplete files will not be reviewed. Further, if multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. All submitted items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Inquiries: Corie O. Fulgencio-Arre, HR Specialist; mcfa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529231/casual-hire-assistant-marketing-director": {
      "title": "Casual Hire – Assistant Marketing Director",
      "description": "Casual Hire – Assistant Marketing Director Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02292 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 10/28/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire – Assistant Marketing Director Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Sports Marketing Date Posted : October 26, 2026 Closing Date : October 28, 2026 Full Time / Part Time : Temporary Full-Time Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, hours not to exceed 40 hours per week. 89 day appointment. Duties and Responsibilities Under the direct supervision of the Marketing Director, assist the office with generating revenue, increasing attendance, and enhancing the overall game day experience for the University of Hawai‘i Athletics Department. Develop, write, and execute comprehensive marketing and promotional plans for assigned sports with a primary focus on maximizing ticket revenue and driving attendance. Assist in the creation and placement of traditional and digital advertising (radio, print, TV, social media). Write and manage detailed game day scripts, PA announcements, and video board rundowns. Coordinate and execute on-field/on-court promotions, halftime shows, sponsor recognitions, and special presentations. Direct game day marketing staff and interns to ensure a high-energy fan experience. Work closely with the Media Relations and Digital Media teams to conceptualize engaging social media content, graphics, and video campaigns that align with the department's voice. Execute social media posts to aid in promotion of upcoming UH Athletics related events. Work side-by-side with the department’s multimedia rights partner to ensure all contracted corporate sponsorship elements are fulfilled during events. Responsible to work scheduled UH Athletics related events to promote UH Athletics which may require flexible work availability during nights, weekends and holidays. Assists with the recruitment of student workers (i.e. paid, interns, volunteers). Oversee and manage student workers on a day to day basis. Demonstrate a positive and professional working attitude and maintain a collaborative working relationships with the coaching staff, team members, support staff, administrators in the UH Athletics Department, internal and external organizations, groups, and individuals and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, and other individuals interested in Athletics. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Know, recognize, and comply with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if you have reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees or students, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree. Some experience working for a company or organization in marketing, advertising, and/or sales. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain e?ective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Ability to work cooperatively and e?ectively with people of diverse backgrounds. Must be able to work nights, weekends and holidays as needed. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to exercise discretion and sound analyses to a variety of administrative support, program management or related issues. Any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, and 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries : Derek Inouchi, Associate Athletics Director for Strategic Communication, inouchi@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
