    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5499807/research-associate-patient-navigator-0097716t-readvertisement": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Patient Navigator) - (0097716T) - READVERTISEMENT",
      "description": "Research Associate (Patient Navigator) - (0097716T) - READVERTISEMENT Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa - UH Cancer Center, Kaka'ako - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02071 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Opening Date 10/20/2026 Closing Date 10/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 00997716T Description Benefits Description Title: Research Associate (Patient Navigator) – (0097716T) - READVERTIEMENT Position Number: 0097716T Hiring Unit: RES & DEAN OF GRAD DIV, CANCER CT HI, CANCER CT HI Location: Honolulu, Kaka'ako, Hawai'i Date Posted: October 20, 2026 Closing Date: October 30, 2026 at 11:59 P.M. Band : B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBB, minimum $5, 640/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities: *Provides patient navigation and cancer care access support to patients, families, caregivers, and healthcare providers for the Susan C. Hirano Cancer C.A.R.E. Community and the Ka Umeke Lama Medical Oncology Fellowship Program at the University of Hawaii Cancer Center. *Provides administrative, operational, and programmatic support to the planning, coordination, and implementation of activities associated with the Susan C. Hirano Cancer C.A.R.E. Community and the Ka Umeke Lama Medical Oncology Fellowship Program. *Serves as the primary point of contact for individuals seeking information about cancer diagnosis, treatment options, second opinions, clinical trials, supportive care services, and community resources. Serves as a trusted resource for patients and families navigating complex healthcare systems and seeking access to cancer-related services. *Conducts intake assessments to identify patient and family needs, barriers to care, and resource gaps. Provides information and guidance regarding available cancer treatment providers, healthcare facilities, specialty services, clinical trial opportunities, supportive care programs, transportation assistance, financial resources, survivorship services, and other relevant support systems. *Maintains current knowledge of local, national, and international cancer care resources, clinical trial databases, and referral networks. Assists individuals in understanding how to access clinical trial information and facilitates connections with appropriate healthcare providers and research institutions when requested. *Coordinates referrals and follow-up communication with community organizations, healthcare systems, patient advocacy groups, and research partners. Documents interactions and resource referrals in accordance with program policies and reporting requirements. *Provides educational materials and information while ensuring that all communications remain within the scope of patient navigation and do not constitute medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment recommendations. *Assists with organizing community events, educational workshops, support programs, wellness activities, and other community engagement initiatives. Coordinates event logistics, participant registration, communications, scheduling, and follow-up activities. *Maintains program records, participant databases, attendance tracking, and program evaluation data. Assists with preparing reports, collecting participant feedback, and documenting program outcomes to support continuous improvement and donor stewardship efforts. *Collaborates with Cancer Center faculty, staff, community partners, patient advocates, and volunteers to enhance program offerings and strengthen community engagement. *Provides administrative support for fellowship-related events, recruitment activities, reporting requirements, and other programmatic needs as the fellowship program develops and launches. *Collaborates with Ka Umeke Lama leadership and program staff to support efforts aimed at strengthening the oncology workforce and expanding access to cancer care throughout Hawaii and the Pacific region. Other duties as assigned. *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Nursing, Science or health-related field and three (3) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for patient navigation, public health, clinical research, oncology support or equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of methods and procedures in the area of healthcare systems methods and procedures, oncology care delivery, patient navigation principles, community-based resources, and health equity issues affecting access to cancer care Considerable working knowledge and understanding of Federal and State laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with governing of human subjects research and familiarity with clinical trial operations and regulatory requirements. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Supplemental Qualifications: Familiarity with medical terminology and the challenges faced by individuals navigating cancer diagnosis and treatment is desirable. Specialized knowledge of specific cancer care sites conducting clinical trials and/or of areas such as education/training, regulatory, investigational drug accountability/storage, specimen collection/processing, database maintenance/reporting and auditing. Demonstrated knowledge in clinical trials, oncology research, and healthcare systems. Demonstrated ability to interact positively with clinical study participants and others involved with the research environment. Desirable Qualifications: Master's Degree from an accredited college or university in a health-related field, such as Nursing, Medical Records, Medical Technology or Pharmacology. Working knowledge of the University of Hawai`i (UH), the RCUH, and UHCC policies and procedures. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen and attach the required documents. Applicants must submit 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2) resume, 3) names and contact information of three (3) professional references and 4) official transcripts (OR original documentation from the institution confirming degree OR copy of U.S. board certification). Copy of transcript is acceptable in place of U.S. board certification for evaluation purposes but original is required upon hire. Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA will be required before date of hire. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant (Note: Only complete applications will be considered.) NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: hr@cc.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Cancer Center Address 701 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5521975/research-associate-senior-0096615t": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Senior (0096615T)",
      "description": "Research Associate, Senior (0096615T) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02247 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - OVPRS - Undergraduate Research Opportunities Opening Date 10/20/2026 Closing Date 11/4/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096615T Description Benefits Description Title: Research Associate, Senior Position Number: 0096615T Hiring Unit: OFFICE OF THE PROVOST, OFC OF VP FOR RES & SCHOLAR, OFC OF VP FOR RES & SCHOLAR Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: October 20, 2026 Closing Date: November 4, 2026 Band: B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Position description pending final approval. Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Collaborates with the UROP Director in the overall administration of all Undergraduate Research Opportunities Program (UROP) activities, including policy and program development, resource/funding allocation, and program implementation. 2. *Collaborates with UROP Director to lead program assessment, reporting, strategic planning, and to identify, pursue and secure funding opportunities on and off campus. 3. *Oversees and manages procedures for processing of funded proposals & student records for UROP including enrollment verification, application submission, and deliberation. 4. *Supervises management of awards, electronic files, confidential records, and ensures award funding is disbursed and that award recipients fulfill all requirements for awards to provide students with enrichment opportunities through research and creative work activities. 5. *Collaborates with the Program Coordinator in the design and implementation of the Summer Undergraduate Research Experience (SURE) program for undergraduate students at UH Manoa, including professional development modules, a faculty mentoring grant opportunity and an end-of-summer venue for undergraduate students to present their research/creative work in a public forum. 6. *Manages accounting and disbursement of scholarship funds and the overall UROP budget throughout the academic year via sound budget planning and oversight to fully maximize the available budget in a given fiscal year. 7. *Manages UROP's Undergraduate Research Opportunities faculty council membership and facilitates meetings, agendas, reference materials, and proposal review process. 8. *Evaluates progress/performance of UROP and adjusts planning and implementation in consultation with the Director to ensure mission support and that program needs are met. 9. *Strategizes and oversees campus-wide promotion of UROP, including direct engagement with individual academic units. 10. Evaluates the funding application process on a continuing basis to improve program efficiency and the student application experience. 11.*Facilitates faculty mentoring of students by collating and disseminating faculty mentoring resources, administering the faculty mentor eligibility petition process, recruiting new faculty mentors, and recognizing excellence in mentorship. 12. *Provides guidance to faculty mentors and program administrators on undergraduate research best practices. 13. *Actively supports policies and practices that advance a diverse, inclusive, and accessible undergraduate research community for all students, staff, and faculty. 14.*Represents UROP and the Office of the Vice Provost for Research and Scholarship at events, conferences, presentations, information sessions, and meetings. 15. *Oversees the implementation of special events and research/creative activities, including workshops and events. 16. *Develops and maintains collegial and professional relationships with individuals and departments within UHM, and with partners from external institutions/organizations. 17. *Participates in professional development workshops/conferences to stay current on best practices in research/creative work mentoring, undergraduate research/creative work, and educational technology. 18. *Maintains affiliations with national-level professional associations including the Council on Undergraduate Research. 19. Practices sound decision making to optimize student service and office efficiency. 20. Manages, hires, trains, and supervises administrative staff and graduate/student assistants, overseeing office duties and monitoring tasks for UROP 21. Other Duties as Assigned. * Denotes Essential Function Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Education, Natural Sciences, Social Sciences, Humanities or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for coordinating and promoting academic and/or research training programs in higher education; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of coordinating and promoting of academic and/or research training programs as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with research, student services, and office procedures in higher education. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective and collegial working relationships with students and faculty, and internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals from a diverse, multicultural student population with superb interpersonal relationship skills. 10. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. 11. Demonstrated success working in a team environment. 12. Ability to manage and promote change when required, while being flexible and creative in meeting challenges in evolving work situations. 13. Excellent analytical, problem solving and decision making skills. 14. Ability to communicate and make presentations in a clear and concise fashion. 15. Skills to leverage data and student success technologies to make informed, timely decisions 16. Knowledge of research/creative endeavors. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience supporting undergraduate research programming on a university campus. 2. Experience working with University academic or research programs. 3. Familiarity with competitive grant and award programs, including collaborating and/or writing proposals for grants, gifts, and/or contracts. 4. Experience conducting and disseminating independent research/scholarship/creative activity. 5. Experience writing for scholarly publication and successful grant applications. 6. Knowledge of best practices (CUR) in undergraduate research programming. 7. Advanced knowledge of the diverse populations on a university campus and the various opportunities and potential needs presented by a diverse student population. To Apply Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicants must submit the following: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2. Resume or CV ; 3. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of 3 professional references ; 4. Copy of unofficial transcript (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries will NOT be accepted. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted.) Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. I MPORTANT: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please DO NOT include any self-identifying photos, REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE on any documents within the application packet. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. Incomplete or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Inquiries: Creighton Litton, 808-956-6004 litton@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
