    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5525106/assistant-professor-school-counseling-0085184-0082827": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085184 & 0082827)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085184 & 0082827) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02268 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education Division Educational Psychology Opening Date 10/21/2026 Position Number: 0085184 & 0082827 Description Title: Assistant Professor - School Counseling (0085184 & 0082827) Position Number: 0085184 & 0082827-2 positions Hiring Unit: College of Education, Department of Educational Psychology (EDEP) Location: Manoa Date Posted: October 21, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins December 2, 2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Tenure Track Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: General funds, contingent upon position clearance. Appointment to begin August 2027 or soon thereafter. Selectee is required to complete the COE New Faculty Orientation in the first year, successfully pass a criminal background check, fulfill all mandatory training and compliance requirements and maintain an on-campus presence. One of the selectees may be appointed as the Director of School Counseling, an 11-month position. The Director appointment is for one-year and may be renewed annually. The Director is responsible for program oversight, administration, accreditation, and strategic growth. The second position will coordinate and support the practicum and internship components of the program. Duties and Responsibilities Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate classes in School Counseling and Educational Psychology in face-to-face, online and/or hybrid formats, as determined by departmental needs. Plan and implement the School Counseling program, including developing course syllabi, course scheduling, and program assessments. Coordinate field practicum and internship placements, preparing and supervising field practicum supervisors. Plan and implement program recruitment, admission and retention efforts. Advise and mentor School Counseling students. Hold regular in-person office hours to support student learning and engagement. Maintain alignment with State and national accrediting agencies and complete accreditation processes. Participate in accreditation processes, including documentation and report preparation. Coordinate with the Office of Student Academic Services (OSAS) for submission of students’ completion data. Establish and maintain effective work relationships with staff and colleagues. Facilitate partnerships with local schools and school counseling organizations. Utilize excellent oral and written communication and presentation skills. Utilize excellent strategic and analytic thinking skills to solve problems and make decisions. Attend in-person departmental and college meetings. Maintain an active program of research/scholarly activity and publications in area of specialization. Perform service to the department, college, university and community. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications An earned doctorate in Counselor Education, Mental Health Counseling, Educational Psychology, Psychology or closely related field from a college or university of recognized standing; ABD candidates will be considered if the degree is conferred by date of hire. 1 year of experience as a school or mental health counselor. Expertise working with students from varied populations. Evidence of research/scholarly productivity in school counseling or a related area. Desirable Qualifications Holds licensure as a school counselor. 1 year of experience teaching graduate students in school counseling or a related field. 1 year of experience mentoring and supervising students in school counseling field settings. Evidence of successful teaching across a range of school counseling courses. Demonstrated history of successful leadership outcomes in higher education or other educational institutions. Evidence of successful teaching of online or hybrid courses in higher education. Strong working knowledge and understanding of established professional code of ethics and best practices. Understanding of current issues facing school counseling in Hawai‘i. Evidence of partnerships with school and community organizations. Evidence of scholarly activities related to counseling. Publications and grant funding related to research agenda. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2. Curriculum vitae 3. Names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references 4. Official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire) 5. Summaries of student evaluations of higher education courses taught previously 6. 1-3 reprints of selected scholarly publications Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Lois Yamauchi; yamauchi@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531226/assistant-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in AI Computing",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in AI Computing Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 0085094-02 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0085094 Description Title: Assistant Professor in AI Computing Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: 0085094 Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type : 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in AI Computing, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes an Assistant Professor position in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems and an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who advance AI algorithms, models, software, and computing platforms. Areas of interest include machine learning algorithms and architectures; generative AI, large language models, and multimodal foundation models; AI methods for scientific computing and discovery; scalable and resource-efficient training and inference; distributed and accelerated AI computing; hardware-software co-design; embedded and edge AI; and AI data and software infrastructure. We welcome theoretical, methodological, and systems research that improves AI capabilities, efficiency, or scalability while supporting human judgment and beneficial use. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer science Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program Publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduates Participate in capstone mentoring Develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. Strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant Professor search. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Note: If this is your first time using NEOGOV you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531228/assistant-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 20261027 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 20261027 Description Title: Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: Pending Position Number Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes an Assistant Professor position in AI Computing, and an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who develop AI systems capable of reasoning, planning, using tools, and collaborating with people. Areas of interest include planning and sequential decision-making; learning and reasoning for autonomous agents; embodied and physical AI; interaction with external data and software; workflow orchestration; multi-agent coordination; memory and adaptation; and human-AI collaboration and oversight. We welcome research on agent behavior and the design and evaluation of trustworthy autonomous systems that preserve human agency and operate effectively in real-world environments. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer sciences Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program; publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduates Participate in capstone mentoring Develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service; and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. Strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant Professor search. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Note: If this is your first time using NEOGOV you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531225/assistant-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 0084029-02 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0084029 Description Title: Assistant or Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: 0084029 Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes two Assistant Professor positions, one in AI Computing and one in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who advance the foundations, methods, and computing systems needed for safe, trustworthy, resilient, and human-first AI. Areas of interest include AI safety and alignment; security and privacy of AI models, privacy-preserving AI systems; verification, validation, and monitoring; dependable embedded and edge AI; resilient cyber-physical and intelligent sensing systems; and human-centered evaluation and responsible deployment. Contributions may address ethical, legal, and societal implications of AI and malicious attacks, failures, uncertainty, or resource constraints through AI methods, enabling computing systems, or their integration. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer sciences Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program; publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduate Participate in capstone mentoring; develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service; and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. For Assistant Professor appointments: strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. For Associate Professor consideration: an established independent research program, sustained scholarly contributions and extramural funding, and a record of effective teaching, student mentoring, and service appropriate to the rank. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant or Associate Professor search; rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and subject to applicable University appointment requirements. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531207/casual-hire-business-office-and-uh-foundation-fiscal-assistant": {
      "title": "Casual Hire – Business Office and UH Foundation Fiscal Assistant",
      "description": "Casual Hire – Business Office and UH Foundation Fiscal Assistant Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02304 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date 10/30/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: Pending Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire – Business Office and UH Foundation Fiscal Assistant Hiring Unit: Athletics Department Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date: October 30, 2026 Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: This is a shared position, split between University of Hawaii at Manoa (UHM) Athletics and the University of Hawaii Foundation. Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, 89 day appointment, not to exceed 40 hours/week. Duties and Responsibilities UHM Athletics Business Office Assist the Assistant Athletics Director for Business Operations with providing administrative and fiscal support to the coaches and staff in coordination with the Business Office Staff and in compliance with all University policies and State and Federal Laws. Reviews and reconciles Honolulu Police Department/Sheriff and sport official invoices and executes payment documents to individual external offices. Coordinates and prepares invoices for staffing, business operations, scholarships, postage, copier, facility rentals and other billings. Creates Disbursement Vouchers for monthly phone stipends for athletic department staff payments. Reviews quotes for coaches/staff family travel in accordance with contracts and executes payment documents for invoices. Reviews and reconciles monthly revenue sharing payments made through Kuali Financial Services (KFS) and Teamworks Wallet to ensure accuracy and completeness University of Hawaii Foundation Receives day-to-day direction from the Associate Director of Athletic Engagement for UH Foundation-related responsibilities. Assists with providing administrative support for the Na Koa Football Club and Booster Club. Processes invoices and payment requests through Concur for Na Koa Football Club and UH Athletics booster club events, operations, and related expenses. Prepares and submits financial transmittals for donations, event revenue, sponsorships, and other payments received. Monitors outstanding invoices, pending reimbursements, and financial transactions to ensure timely processing and reconciliation. Creates, manages, and monitors Eventbrite pages for events, including ticket sales, attendee registrations, and event reporting. Reviews and reconciles event revenues and expenses to ensure accurate financial reporting and documentation. Maintains accurate financial records, registration reports, receipts, payment confirmations, and supporting documentation. Coordinates with UH Foundation staff and booster club representatives to ensure timely processing of payments, deposits, and other financial transactions. Provides administrative and on-site support for Na Koa Football Club and UH Athletics booster club events as needed, including post-event administrative responsibilities. Assists with collecting and processing event payments, donations, and credit card transactions through approved payment systems in accordance with UH Foundation policies and procedures. Ensures compliance with applicable UH Foundation financial procedures, policies, and documentation requirements. Additional Duties Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, donor information, financial records, and other sensitive information, in accordance with federal and state laws , UH Foundation policies and procedures and University of Hawaii policies. Maintains collaborative working relationships with administrators and coaches in the department, other college offices, and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, UH Foundation staff and other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. Knows, recognizes, and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there are reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees or students, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in business administration, humanities, social sciences, natural sciences or related field and some administrative/fiscal experience in an intercollegiate setting or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in business administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with business administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the business administration environment. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. Ability to work nights, weekends, and holidays as required. Ability to work effectively and independently under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to adapt/respond to different types of customers. Desirable Qualifications: Experience with University of Hawaii on-line systems, including, but not limited to Kuali Financial System and eTravel. Experience with University of Hawaii Foundation financial systems. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries : Tiffany Kuraoka, Assistant Athletics Director for Business Operations, tkuraoka@hawaii.edu or Hunter Niswander, Director of Development and NIL, Hunter.Niswander@uhfoundation.org EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5128131/educational-specialist-simulation-lab-tech-0079534t": {
      "title": "Educational Specialist (Simulation Lab Tech) 0079534T",
      "description": "Educational Specialist (Simulation Lab Tech) 0079534T Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2025-02264 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene - Dean's Office Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0079534T Description Title: Educational Specialist (Simulation Lab Technician) Position Number: 0079534T Hiring Unit: School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene (Translational Health Science Simulation Center) Location: Manoa Campus / Honolulu Date Posted: October 27, 2026 (Readvertisement) Closing Date: Recruitment is open until filled. Band: A Minimum Monthly Salary: Salary Schedules and Placement Information Full Time/Part Time: Full-Time, 100% Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: This position will support the operations of the University of Hawaii at Manoa School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene (SONDH) UH Translational Health Science Simulation Center (THSSC). The UH THSSC brings real-life scenarios to the classroom so that students can gain the experience, confidence and skills they need to provide quality care. The center integrates state-of-the-art equipment, electronic patient records and practices currently used by the state’s leading medical centers. Click HERE for a virtual/video tour of the THSSC, and see our simulation techs and students in action for an elevated experience in this state-of-the-art healthcare technology. The position is expected to begin this Spring 2027 semester or as mutually acceptable. Renewal of appointment is dependent on performance, program needs and/or availability of funds. To know more about the UH at Manoa, SONDH, go to: https://nursing.hawaii.edu/ . Duties and Responsibilities: * Serve as Simulation Lab Technician responsible for the set-up, operation, and maintenance of the simulation equipment in the UH Translational Health Science Simulation Center (THSSC). * Operate and control manikin functions; provide verbal responses in scenarios; assist with simulated patient sessions; troubleshoot technical issues in real-time; and other duties to ensure smooth operation of the simulation experience. * Clean and maintain equipment, supplies and facility after simulation sessions. * Assist simulation specialists with the demonstration of THSSC equipment including simulators and the standardized patient program. * Assist THSSC team members with integrating new equipment and software into the functions of the Center. * Provide technical support to simulation specialists in the training and orientation of internal and external users. * Assist with programming scenarios into simulator equipment, collaborate with team to revise existing scripts to correspond to the goals of a particular session and/or audience's level of experience, and develops directions for each simulator instructor. * Prepare facility, medical supplies, materials and instruments, and equipment for simulation sessions for High Fidelity, Low Fidelity (dry lab), standardized patients, faculty and others. * Manage and maintain inventories of simulators, equipment, and supplies, and keep software and hardware configurations updated. * Assist with the annual and mid-year equipment preventive maintenance and inventory for THSSC. * Conduct educational tours of THSSC. Participate in departmental strategic planning and orientation/training of new THSSC staff. Maintains files of simulation cases and modules. Other duties as assigned. (*) Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in education, social science, communications, education technology, healthcare or related field and one (1) year of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for customer service in a laboratory setting, training, or operations support in an education setting, health care; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in training, service-oriented occupations or operational support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with training, interpersonal relationships, or operational support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the training, servicing situations, or operational support environment. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. Demonstrated ability to acquire new skills and learn new techniques to operate technology and equipment in a health care training and research environment. Demonstrated ability to problem-solve and make quick, decisive decisions in a changing health training and research environment. Desirable Qualifications: Experience with simulation learning specifically with high-fidelity, manikin and standardized/simulated patient learning. Experience working with students and faculty in an academic or research environment Familiarity with technology resources utilized in learning or research centers such as computer-based equipment, data bases, and audio visual systems. Experience providing logistical facility support, such as class, laboratory, meeting, or other activity set-up. Familiarity with medical instruments and equipment used in medical facility. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references; and Copies of unofficial transcripts (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries and copy of diplomas are NOT ACCEPTABLE. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. NOTE: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos they will be redacted. PLEASE REDACT Social Security #s and birth dates on transcript copies. All submitted items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. No late applications will be accepted. Inquiries: Corie O. Fulgencio-Arre, HR Specialist; mcfa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or adpaper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act(ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529543/graduate-assistant-battery-data-analysis": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant - Battery Data Analysis",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant - Battery Data Analysis Location UH Manoa - SOEST, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02295 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - SOEST - Hawai'i Natural Energy Institute Opening Date 10/26/2026 Position Number: 0000GA11 Description Title: Graduate Assistant Position Number: 0000GA11 Hiring Unit: Hawai’i Natural Energy Institute Location: Manoa Date Posted: October 26, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous – first review of applications begins November 4, 2026 Monthly Type: 11 Month Full Time / Part Time: Part Time Temporary / Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Extramural funds. To begin approximately January 4, 2027 or soon thereafter. Renewal of appointment is contingent upon availability of funds, program/operational needs and satisfactory performance. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist the Principal Investigator in conducting research on: generation of battery synthetic datasets, implementation of machine learning algorithms for battery diagnosis and prognosis, and maintaining python libraries and webapp. Minimum Qualifications: Master of Science in Mechanical Engineering or equivalent. Prior admission to the Mechanical Engineering graduate program at the University of Hawai’i Manoa. Working knowledge of principles and practices of scientific research. Applied data science experience. Experience in Python. Desirable Qualifications: Battery data analysis experience. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: 1. cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2. current curriculum vitae, 3. statement of research interests, 4. names of three (3) professional references with contact information (including email addresses), and 5. official transcripts (copies accepted, however official transcripts will be required upon hire). Incomplete applications will not be considered and all documentation become the property of the University of Hawaii at Manoa. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Matthieu Dubarry at (808) 956-2349 or matthieu@hawaii.edu. EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - SOEST - Hawai'i Natural Energy Institute Address 1680 East-West Road, POST 109 Honolulu, Hawaii, 96822 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5521940/graduate-research-assistant-0000ga11-up-to-2-positions": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11)-up to 2 positions",
      "description": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11)-up to 2 positions Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02245 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Thompson School of Social Work & Public Health - DPHS Opening Date 10/19/2026 Closing Date 11/9/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0000GA11 Description Title: Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11)-up to 2 positions Position Number: 0000GA11 Hiring Unit: Department of Public Health Sciences Location: University of Hawai?i at Manoa Date Posted: October 19, 2026 Closing Date: November 9, 2026, 11:59 PM Hawai?i Time Salary Information: GA Step 17 (11-month) or comparable to qualifications and experience, see GA Compensation Schedule Monthly Type: 11-month Full Time Equivalency (FTE): .50 Job Term: Non-Tenure/Part-Time/Temporary Funding: Extramural Other Conditions: pending position clearance and availability of funds. position renewable contingent to availability of funds, program needs, and satisfactory performance. position to tentatively begin in December 2026 or soon thereafter. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist the principal investigator and project team with coordination and implementation of suicide prevention trainings, programming, and evaluation. Assist with communication and coordination with/among project partners (e.g., healthcare, K-12 education, community based organizations, youth groups, etc.). Assist with preparation and submission of reports and manuscripts relating to mental health and suicide prevention, including conducting literature searches and formatting data tables. Assist with development and dissemination of public health messaging products (e.g., brochures, website content, etc.) relating to mental health/suicide prevention awareness. Participate in community trainings, events, and forums to promote mental health/suicide prevention for the public (some may require travel to community sites, including neighbor islands, pending university travel restrictions). Participate in meetings, conference/video calls, and trainings for the project. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Classified graduate student at the University of Hawai'i at Manoa. Be in good academic standing and maintain a GPA of 3.0 or above. Maintain 6 credits per semester, with the exception of those who have reached 800 (dissertation) or 700F (thesis). Able to work respectfully with diverse communities, and with a heightened level of sensitivity necessary for mental health/suicide prevention work. Able to work and time-manage independently, under direction of the principal investigator and project team, and with appropriate training. Prior experience conducting literature searches via online academic search engines (e.g., Pubmed, Psycinfo, etc.). Working knowledge of computers and basic processing (e.g., Microsoft Suite including Word, Excel, Powerpoint) and connectivity programs (e.g., Zoom, Webex, Teams). Must have strong skills with respect to communication (oral and written), organization, and attention-to-detail. Have access to transportation for visits/meetings with community sites. Must be personally ready/able to work in suicide prevention (those who have recently been impacted by suicide loss, or are personally struggling, sometimes find it difficult to engage in suicide prevention work). Desirable Qualifications: University of Hawai'i graduate student in public health, social work, psychology, or related field. Knowledge or experience in the fields of mental health and/or suicide prevention. Prior experience writing and submitting IRB protocols/applications, agency/technical reports, and/or peer-reviewed manuscripts. Prior experience developing/designing public health awareness products (e.g., brochures, website and social media content, etc.). Prior experience on projects that required interfacing with diverse populations, community partners, and the general public. Able to commit to project for at least one year. To Apply: Click on the \"APPLY\" button to complete an application and attach the following REQUIRED documents/information: current curriculum vitae; cover letter describing how you meet the minimum and desirable qualifications; names and contact information (including email addresses) of at least 3 professional references; and transcripts or equivalent documents from institution confirming highest educational degree relevant to this position (unofficial copies acceptable, official originals required upon hire ). Note: First time NEOGOV users are required to create an account. Only applications through NEOGOV are accepted. Inquiries: Dr. Jeanelle Sugimoto-Matsuda, Principal Investigator, jsugimot@hawaii.edu . EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531206/it-specialist-0077185t": {
      "title": "IT Specialist (0077185T)",
      "description": "IT Specialist (0077185T) Location UH Manoa - College of Ed, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02303 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education - Center on Disability Studies Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date 11/12/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0077185T Description Title: IT Specialist Position Number: 0077185T Hiring Unit: C OF EDUC, CENTER ON DISABILITY STUDIES Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date: November 12, 2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Extramural funds. Appointment to begin November 2026 or soon thereafter. Renewal contingent upon satisfactory performance, department needs, and availability of funds. Duties and Responsibilities *Lead the Center on Disability Studies (CDS) IT team to work with faculty, staff, and students to develop a system to improve, manage and maintain CDS websites, IT network, hardware and software, and other equipment and devices. *Oversee the management of project websites and ITE network at the Center and advise the Director/Associate Director and Principal Investigators (PI) on its operations. Work with other IT staff to implement, administer, and maintain servers, directory services, file serving, databases, and website development. Oversee other IT staff in managing and maintaining the overall operations of fixed and mobile computer labs. Provide physical, hardware, and software maintenance for audio/visual equipment, computer and networking hardware, and peripheral devices. *Evaluate and assess the needs of the unit by discussing with the Director/Associate Director and PIs. Make recommendations for improvement. *Develop a system of support in resolving IT issues. Design and create manuals/protocols based on the needs of the unit and projects. Oversee the maintenance and management of wired and wireless Local Area Network (LAN) infrastructure for offices and other shared meeting spaces. Conduct IT related trainings including accessibility trainings. Supervise and manage the workload assignments of subordinate IT staff, undergraduate student assistants, and casual hires. Oversee IT staff in the installation of new equipment, and life-cycling management. Attend professional development courses or seminars as necessary. Keep current on current and emerging operating systems for Mac OS, iOS, Chrome OS, Android, and Windows as it their operability to the unit's mission and goals. *Keep abreast on the current policies and accessibility matters such as on Section 508 and assistive technologies. *Follows and implements directives and guidance relatedto best practices from University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Services. *Ensures the consistent adoption, implementation, and enforcement of recommendations issued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service. *Keeps abreast of recommendationsissued through University of Hawai'i System Information Technology Service, and takes timely action as needed. *Continuously monitor and lead initiatives to enhance system reliability, security, and operational efficiency. Supervise and mentor IT staff to assure that administrative directives and industry best practices are understood and followed. Performs other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a pertinent baccalaureate educational degree in educational technology, management information systems or related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional information technology experience with responsibilities for IT support services, technology asset management, systems maintenance, and/or IT related training, of which 2 years of the experience must have been comparable in scope and complexity to the next lower pay band in the University of Hawai'i broadband system; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of management information systems and educational technology as demonstrated by the broad knowledge and understanding of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving information technology concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of the broad technology, systems, hardware and software associated with educational and assistive technologies. Demonstrated ability to recognize a wide range of intricate problems, use reasoning and logic to determine accurate causes, and apply principles and practices to determine, evaluate, integrate, and implement practical and thorough solutions in an effective and timely manner. Proven ability to comprehend, interpret and implement administrative directives and guidance to ensure IT operations align with organizational standards and industry best practices. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Strong understanding of IT service management, cybersecurity principles, risk management, and compliance requirements. Demonstrated experience implementing and maintaining IT best practices, standards, and governance. Considerable working knowledge of helpdesk or technical services management. Excellent interpersonal skills including customer service and collaboration with technical personnel. Knowledge of various electronic accessing methods, including file servers and services, databases, and Internet-related services such as web servers and clients. Proficiency in using Google Applications (e.g. Docs, Sheets, Drive, etc.). Proficiency with Microsoft Office products (e.g. Word, PowerPoint, Excel, etc.). Desirable Qualifications Working knowledge and experience in managing and deploying SaaS (Software as a Service). Working knowledge and demonstrated experience in applying and adhering to usability and accessibility standards as it applies to technology. Demonstrated ability to develop clean, structured websites and conduct thorough testing and validation of website functionality and accessibility. Considerable working knowledge of the Adobe Suite (e.g. Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, Premiere) to create graphics, animations, audio and videos for use on websites, social media, digital marketing, and other advertising platforms. Working knowledge of deploying devices via an MDM (Mobile Device Management) system. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents to the online application. The applicants should submit the following documents:1. cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, 2. Resume, 3. names and contact information (including e-mail address) of at least 3 professional references, and 4. official transcripts (copies are acceptable with application; official transcripts are required upon hire). Late, incomplete, or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Please REDACT references to social security numbers and birth date on submitted documents. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. NOTE: If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. The maximum file size to upload/attach is 10 MB. If the attachment is larger than 10 MB, please split the file and upload it as a supplemental attachment. Inquiries: Meghan Miller; miller95@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Education - Center on Disability Studies Address 1410 Lower Campus Road, #171 F Honolulu, Hawaii, 96826 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529434/publications-specialist-editorial-design-and-production-manager-0080114": {
      "title": "Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) (0080114)",
      "description": "Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) (0080114) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02293 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - University of Hawai'i Press Opening Date 10/26/2026 Closing Date 12/7/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0080114 Description Title: Publications Specialist (Editorial, Design, and Production Manager) Position Number: 0080114 Hiring Unit: UNIVERSITY OF HAWAII AT MANOA LIBRARY SERVICES, UNIVERSITY OF HAWAII PRESS, EDITORIAL, DESIGN, AND PRODUCTION DEPARTMENT Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa, UH Press (2840 Kolowalu Street, Honolulu, HI 96822) Date Posted: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 12/07/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions) *Serves as the department head and primary representative for the Editorial, Design, and Production (EDP) Department. Manages the department's budget, planning, and organizational development. Manages the manufacturing and digital production workflow across multiple channels for new and backlist titles, ensuring high-quality execution of copyediting, proofreading, design, typesetting, print manufacture, and distribution. *Oversees planning for the the UH Press list by formulating budget estimates, tracking production costs, and executing final project closings for each book. *Establishes, documents, and maintains post-acquisition editorial and production standards while leading process optimization, communicating workflow changes, and ensuring procedural compliance across the department. Serves as the primary cross-functional liaison between internal staff, freelancers, and manufacturing vendors to facilitate a seamless printing and production process. *Directs the financial and operational workflow for each book project by generating accurate cost estimates, establishing and tracking budgets, and executing final project reconciliations. Drives strategic vendor management by negotiating and overseeing contracts for composition and external services, while routing individual projects based on cost, schedule, and vendor expertise. Identifies and pursues cost-savings optimization across all Editorial, Design, and Production (EDP) responsibilities to enhance both fiscal efficiency and final project quality. *Establishes long-term strategies and short-term departmental goals that balance editorial, production, marketing, and sales priorities against shifting industry trends. Advances operational excellence by developing and maintaining internal tools, templates, and systems that maximize EDP efficiency, consistency, and quality. *Conducts comparative analyses of vendors, peer presses, and industry standards: adapts workflows for print, digital, and e-formats while maintaining technical expertise in emerging hardware, software, and AI. Collaborates with the digital publishing manager to ensure departmental workflows plan for strict compliance with evolving accessibility regulations, including the ADA, Section 508, and the EU Accessibility Act. *Manages the daily operations of professional and technical design and production staff, alongside freelance providers, authors, and copyeditors, by assigning duties, setting performance goals, and providing constructive feedback. *Reports directly to the Press Director as an active member of the Press leadership team. Collaborates across departments including acquisitions, marketing, sales, journals, business, and digital publishing to advance shared organizational priorities. Communicates operational status, potential risks, and resource needs regularly to Press leadership. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in humanities or social sciences or related field and 6 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for book editorial design and production, and/or manufacturing management; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of book editorial design and production, and/or manufacturing management; as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with copyright and intellectual property as related the book publishing industry. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, including Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel, and Adobe. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Familiarity with The Chicago Manual of Style and standard scholarly publishing conventions. At least 2 years in a supervisory position applying project management principles. Experience in fiscal management, including financial planning, castoffs and estimates, and preparing reports for management. Experience selecting, contracting, and managing vendor relationships, including negotiating terms, evaluating vendor performance, and routing work to appropriate vendors based on project requirements. Comprehensive knowledge of the technology and standard tools used across editorial, design , production, and digital workflows, including Creative Suite, XML editors, and editorial markup tools sufficient to valuate work and communicate effectively with staff and vendors. Desirable Qualifications Familiarity with UH procurement systems, rules and regulations. Advanced knowledge of Adobe InDesign and Photoshop. Knowledge of printing, digital technologies, and publishing procedures, including short-run digital printing, print on demand, EPUB, and accessibility standards. Working knowledge of accessibility laws and standards relevant to publishing (e.g., ADA, Section 508, EU Accessibility Act) and their implications for design, production, and digital deliverables. Proficiency with BiblioLive or comparable relational database application. To Apply Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents: (1) Cover letter that addresses how the applicant meets the minimum qualifications: degree requirements, experience, knowledge, skills, and abilities, as well as the desirable qualifications; (2) resume; (3) names and contact information (telephone numbers and email addresses) of at least three professional references; and, (4) official transcript(s) showing degree and coursework to date appropriate to the position (copies of official transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to completion of hiring). Late and/or incomplete (applications that do not include all four documents) will not be considered. All documents become the property of the school. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Clem Guthro, University Librarian and Interim Director and Publisher, UH Press, guthroc@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5529171/research-support-staff-0097631t-ctahr-adsc": {
      "title": "Research Support Staff (0097631T; CTAHR/ADSC)",
      "description": "Research Support Staff (0097631T; CTAHR/ADSC) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02291 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date 11/9/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097631T Description Benefits Questions Description Title : Research Support Staff (CTAHR/ADSC) Position Number(s): 0097631T Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Agricultural Diagnostic Service Center (ADSC) Location: CARES Wailua, Kauai County; Kapaa, HI Date Posted : October 27, 2026 Closing Date : November 9, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Temporary Other Conditions : This position is subject to availability of funding and renewal is contingent upon satisfactory performance, staffing requirements, and/or availability of funding. All application materials should be submitted by November 9 , 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: *Coordinate technical logistics for setting up and maintaining field and laboratory trials evaluating crop performance, soil responses, and pest management systems as it relates to full cycle seed production. *Oversee scientific data logging systems and collect environmental parameters, soil metrics, water quality data, and plant growth measurements using scientific field instruments. *Coordinate the collection, cataloging, and preparation of soil, plant tissue, water, and microorganism samples for analytical laboratory processing. *Manages field isolation procedures in seed-production plots, monitoring spatial separation standards and buffer zones to safeguard genetic integrity, mitigate outcrossing risks, and preserve UH cultivar stock purity. *Oversees comprehensive seed quality assurance testing, managing multi-parameter analytical evaluations, including purity, moisture, and standardized viability trials, to guarantee strict alignment with state regulatory framework and HDOA requirements. *Oversees daily seed distribution logistics, monitoring inter-island shipments across multiple facility sites and leading bulk packaging operations to meet delivery schedules and quality standards. *Perform technical field evaluations to monitor pest/disease incidence and report physiological crop conditions to research project leads. *Log experimental trial parameters, field mapping coordinates, and observation data into specialized research databases and web-based decision tools. *Oversee daily technical operations and environmental schedules for research greenhouses, shade houses, hydroponic units, and/or field plots. *Program, troubleshoot, and calibrate automated irrigation networks, environmental control computers, and specialized research facility equipment. *Coordinate research plot layout schedules, treatment application timing, and technical equipment setup for research trials. *Inspect research facilities, automated environmental controls, and field instrumentation to perform specialized technical maintenance and schedule major facility repairs. *Maintain detailed operational logs, chemical/pesticide application tracking, inventory records, and facility usage schedules. *Manage procurement logistics for specialized research supplies, fertilizers, field tools, and safety gear. *Monitor field and facility compliance with state, federal, and University environmental health, safety, and worker protection standards. *Assist research personnel in compiling trial summary tables, preparing field demonstration plot layouts, and drafting technical documentation. * Support time-critical research trial requirements, crop maintenance, facility operational monitoring, and other research functions, which may require working outside of normal business hours as needed. *Transports equipment and materials for research to various locations. Perform other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Botany, Biology, Horticulture or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for N/A; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of research, data collection, reports, and/or other research support. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and agricultural research, environmental management, and/or program management software. Ability of active listening skills and the ability to work independently across multiple locations, and flexibility to adapt to changing research and/or operational needs. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirements may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Ability to work outside of normal business, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and/or holidays as needed. Ability to lift, carry, or otherwise move supplies and equipment up to 40 pounds, with or without assistance or utilizing appropriate methods (e.g., carts, dollies, or team lifts). Desirable Qualifications Experience supporting agricultural research operations, field trial logistics, greenhouse operations, and/or biological research data collection (or equivalent combination of education and experience). Working knowledge of technical methods in agronomy, horticulture, and/or soil science, to include operation of controlled environment facilities, standard pest and/or disease identification, and/or scientific data logging and/or record-keeping procedures. Ability to coordinate technical field research tasks and maintain accurate data logs. Ability to operate and program automated environmental/irrigation systems. Ability to enforce safety protocols for chemical application and facility operation. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Dr. Kevin Olival, kolival@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ YOUR JOB BENEFITS WITH THE UNIVERSITY OF HAWAI'I The University of Hawai'i is committed to providing benefits that matter to you at each stage of your employment. In addition to competitive salary, we offer a wide variety of resources and benefits programs to our employees, such as a comprehensive health care insurance, pension/retirement program, life insurance, supplemental retirement savings plans, vacation leave, sick leave, paid holidays, child care assistance and education assistance programs. To view a summary of the employee benefits, please visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/documents/2054/ or for more detailed information, please visit: http://www.hawaii.edu/ohr/faculty-staff/benefits 01 Do you have a baccalaureate degree in Botany, Biology, Horticulture or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and all requested document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you attest that you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531226/assistant-professor-in-ai-computing": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in AI Computing",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in AI Computing Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 0085094-02 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0085094 Description Title: Assistant Professor in AI Computing Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: 0085094 Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type : 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in AI Computing, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes an Assistant Professor position in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems and an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who advance AI algorithms, models, software, and computing platforms. Areas of interest include machine learning algorithms and architectures; generative AI, large language models, and multimodal foundation models; AI methods for scientific computing and discovery; scalable and resource-efficient training and inference; distributed and accelerated AI computing; hardware-software co-design; embedded and edge AI; and AI data and software infrastructure. We welcome theoretical, methodological, and systems research that improves AI capabilities, efficiency, or scalability while supporting human judgment and beneficial use. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer science Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program Publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduates Participate in capstone mentoring Develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. Strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant Professor search. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Note: If this is your first time using NEOGOV you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5533243/admin-fiscal-support-specialist-0097671-ctahr-deans-office": {
      "title": "Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0097671, CTAHR/Dean’s Office)",
      "description": "Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0097671, CTAHR/Dean’s Office) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - CTAHR, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02307 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience Division Dean's Office Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/13/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097671 Description Questions Description Title : Admin & Fiscal Support Spec ialist (CTAHR/Dean’s Office) Position Number: 0097671 Hiring Unit: College of Tropical Agriculture and Human Resilience (CTAHR), Office of the Dean Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa; Honolulu, HI Date Posted : October 29, 2026 Closing Date : November 13, 2026, 11:59PM HST Band : A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time : Full-time Month : 11-month Temporary/Permanent : Permanent Other Conditions : All application materials should be submitted by November 13, 2026, 11:59PM HST. Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Please see application instructions. Duties and Responsibilities: ** Provides fiscal and administrative support to assigned department chair on all fiscal and administrative issues. ** May be required to offer departmental support services to multiple units to provided coverage; as required. ** Maintains budget account information in accordance with established requirements to ensure that data is adequate for their intended use and prepares it in a timely manner. ** Assists with preparing short- and long-term financial projections relating to contracts and grants and department operating accounts. ** Collects statistical data to prepare various administrative reports relating to program/project operations and administration. ** Responsible for initiation of fiscal and personnel transactions and providing complete supporting documentation to the Administrative Services offices for review and approval. ** Provides administrative support in the review and preparation of grant proposals and modifications. May be required to provide work assignments to technical personnel and student help; as needed. Other duties as assigned * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities in N/A; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of administrative and/or business management demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with administrative and/or business management. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of administrative and/or business management. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software. TO APPLY: Click in the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and some, if not all, desirable qualifications; Resume/Curriculum vitae (CV); Names of three (3) professional references (contact information including email address); and Official transcripts or original documentation from the institution confirming the degree (copies acceptable; however, official transcripts sent directly from the degree-granting institution are required upon hire). Any confidential data should be redacted (e.g. social security number, date of birth, etc). Diplomas will not be accepted. Note: Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Inquiries : Carol Oshiro, tranc@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ 01 Do you possess a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration or related field? Yes No 02 Complete applications and required document attachments must be submitted before first review and/or close of vacancy. Incomplete applications will not be reviewed by the hiring committee. Do you understand that incomplete applications (as described in the \"To Apply\" section) will not be reviewed by the hiring committee? Yes, I acknowledge that incomplete applications will not be referred or reviewed by the hiring committee. Required Question Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5533346/administrative-officer-96692": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer (96692)",
      "description": "Administrative Officer (96692) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02308 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Hawaiian Knowledge Division Dean's Office Opening Date 10/28/2026 Closing Date 11/12/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096692 Description Title: Administrative Officer Position Number: 0096692 Hiring Unit: HAWAIINUIAKEA SCH HAWN KNOWL, SHK DEAN'S OFF, SHK DEAN'S OFF Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: 10/28/26 Closing Date: 11/12/26 Band: B Salary: Minimum salary $67,680 year/ $5640 month salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other conditions: Pending position clearance Duties and Responsibilities (*Denotes Essential Functions) *Serves as Administrative Officer with procurement and purchasing authority of $15,000. *Responsible for overall fiscal and grants management, including but not limited to: preaward, account establishment and maintenance, budget and budget forecasting, procurement, personnel, disbursing, reporting, and closeout. *Provides administrative staff support and advisement to higher level personnel or organizational groups in the performance of work of substantial scope, difficulty, or complexity in a specialty area. *Prepares pertinent documents including appointment memos, response to internal/external inquiries, legislative requests, etc. Reviews appropriateness of action memos prepared by the School and its programs. *Participates in overall project management and administration of state, federal, and extramural funds allocated to the School. *Prepares, audits, and approves financial/personnel transactions and documentation and ensures compliance with University and Federal (as applicable) rules and regulations. *Performs monthly reconciliation of fiscal documents in order to track account balances. Provides advice and guidance on the most effective and efficient fiscal/personnel methods of managing various programs such as RCUH, ORS, UHF, etc. *Establishes and implements internal procedures for the preparation, processing, and monitoring of fiscal/personnel documents to assure compliance with applicable rules, regulations, and policies. *Maintains and fosters effective working relationships with others both internal and external to the University. *Coordinates personnel and payroll operations; develop and implement internal procedures and make changes as required. *Prepares, reviews, and submits personnel and payroll documents ensuring complete and accurate data input as required by rules and regulations of the University and the State. *Provides first-line guidance and assistance on HR-related matters to staff, faculty, and PIs. *Oversees effort certification reports, and any other reports required by funding agencies. Works with Lead FA on physical inventory and ensures compliance with appropriate State, University, Federal, and/or sponsoring agency requirements. *Manages the department pcard program, providing guidance to user and ensuring compliance. *Provides and/or conducts fiscal compliance training to PIs, managers, and administrative support staff as needed. *Provides administrative support service to higher level professionals who manage programs, grants or contracts. Participates on special teams or committees and prepares plans, reports, and analysis. *Works with senior level professionals in the contract/grant application process including preparing and monitoring grant applications, ensuring proposals meet grant criteria, etc. *Maintains current knowledge of university, state, federal, and, sponsoring agency rules and regulations and ensures faculty and staff are informed of current policy & procedural changes that require immediate implementation. Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in business administration, accounting, finance, human resources, or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for financial management; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of accounting or finance as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with accounting and finance. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Desirable Qualifications Functional knowledge of University and RCUH fiscal and personnel policies and procedures. Experience with the University's on-line fiscal management (KFS, eThority or JASPER), myGrant Research Corporation (fiscal and personnel), SECE and STAR systems. Ability to generate computer applications such as spreadsheet and database programs to meet reporting requirements. Familiarity with the Hawaiinuiakea School of Hawaiian Knowledge programs Professional experience exercising fiscal signing authority. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicant must submit the following: Cover letter that outlines specifically how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, Names with contact information (including email addresses) of three (3) current professional references who can address your qualifications to the advertised duties and responsibilities, Resume, Official or copy of transcripts that reflect degree and course work with CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION (ie. social security number, birth date, etc.) REDACTED. If requested transcripts are unobtainable by review date, please indicate so in your cover letter. Unofficial transcripts are acceptable upon application but official transcripts will be required if selected to fill the position. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Inquiries: hshkhr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5533836/assistant-bookstore-manager-casual": {
      "title": "Assistant Bookstore Manager (Casual)",
      "description": "Assistant Bookstore Manager (Casual) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02314 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Office of Campus Services Division Bookstore System Opening Date 10/28/2026 Closing Date 10/29/2026 4:30 PM Hawaii Position Number: CASUAL Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University of Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees (i.e. paid holidays/leave). Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Working Title: Assistant Bookstore Manager (Casual) Position Number: ****Casual Hire Hiring Unit: UH Office of Campus Services / Bookstore System Location: Various UH Campuses Date Posted: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 10/29/2026, 4:30PM Salary: $26.69/hour Schedule: Temporary Full-Time, up to 89-days Temporary/Permanent: Temporary, Full-Time Funding: Special Other Conditions: N/A Duties and Responsibilities *(Denotes Essential Functions) Serves as Assistant Bookstore Manager at various stores. Assists in managing the daily operations of the bookstore. Work may include evenings, weekends, and holidays, as needed. Responsible for keeping accurate cash counts and controls, reconciling daily sales activity and recording daily sales deposits. Work with manager on any discrepancies in a timely manner. May be assigned to a specific bookstore or may rotate to bookstores at various campuses within the UH Bookstore System. Meetings and training may be held at various locations. Assists faculty with placing textbook orders on a timely basis. Ensures that faculty members are kept advised of the status of their textbook order requests. Aid with the setup of IDAP materials. Follows up on delinquent book orders, damaged books, and delays in shipments. Assists the Bookstore Manager with problem resolution. Aids with timely return of unsold textbooks to publishers and wholesalers. Assists with inventory and sales floor. Ensures timely return of unsold textbooks to publishers. Ensures proper maintenance of shelf stock levels. Supervises the flow of stock replenishment. Monitors outgoing merchandise shipments. Assists with all freight, US mail, UPS, and Federal Express Shipments. Ensures that proper records are maintained, and proper procedures and regulations are followed. Assists in the preparation, pricing, and placement of incoming merchandise and properly displaying merchandise on sales floor. Assists in maintaining an effective floor and fixture plan. Responsible for attractive appearance, cleanliness, and neatness of the sales floor and storage areas. Assists with special programs and projects with other employees, department managers, and branch bookstore managers. Coordinates the efforts of clerical staff and student assistants. Assists student with procuring/assessing their course materials and school supplies. May assist with training and supervision of student assistants, temporary employees, and clerical staff. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Social Science, Arts and Humanities, Education, or related field and 1 year of progressively responsible professional experience, or any equivalent combination or education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills, and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in business administration or related field. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible cases and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in business administration or related field. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. Demonstrated ability to organize work and meet deadlines under strict time constraints. Ability to work beyond normal hours, including evenings and weekends. Ability to travel and work at the various campus bookstores around the state, including the neighbor islands, for an extended period of time. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience in retail in a post-secondary educational bookstore or library. 2. Experience with computerized inventory or database systems and cash register systems. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references; and Copy of your official transcript showing highest degree. (Official transcripts will be required if selected to fill the position.) Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the most recent application will be reviewed. Please not include any self-identifying photos, and PLEASE REDACT REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBER AND BIRTH DATE ON TRANSCRIPT COPIES. All items become the property of the University of Hawai’i Office of Campus Services, and will not be returned. Inquiries: Campus Services Human Resources Office; 808-956-6720, uhmcshr@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531228/assistant-professor-in-agentic-ai-and-autonomous-systems": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 20261027 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 20261027 Description Title: Assistant Professor in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: Pending Position Number Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes an Assistant Professor position in AI Computing, and an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who develop AI systems capable of reasoning, planning, using tools, and collaborating with people. Areas of interest include planning and sequential decision-making; learning and reasoning for autonomous agents; embodied and physical AI; interaction with external data and software; workflow orchestration; multi-agent coordination; memory and adaptation; and human-AI collaboration and oversight. We welcome research on agent behavior and the design and evaluation of trustworthy autonomous systems that preserve human agency and operate effectively in real-world environments. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer sciences Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program; publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduates Participate in capstone mentoring Develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service; and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. Strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant Professor search. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Note: If this is your first time using NEOGOV you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
