    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531225/assistant-associate-professor-in-safe-and-trustworthy-ai-systems": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 0084029-02 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - College of Natural Sciences - ICS Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0084029 Description Title: Assistant or Associate Professor in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems Hiring Unit: College of Natural Sciences, Information & Computer Sciences Department Position: 0084029 Location: Manoa Campus Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date : Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-Month Tenure Track: Tenure Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent The Department of Information and Computer Sciences (ICS) at the University of Hawai?i at Manoa invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position in Safe and Trustworthy AI Systems, beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. This position is part of a three-position cluster focused on Human-First AI that also includes two Assistant Professor positions, one in AI Computing and one in Agentic AI and Autonomous Systems. Each position is a separate search. Candidates who wish to be considered for more than one position must submit a separate application for each. The cluster hire aims to advance research in AI agents, AI computing systems, and AI safety. The candidate will contribute to the department’s growing AI curriculum, the proposed BS in Computer Science Artificial Intelligence Track, and the Professional MCS in AI/Data Science. This position will be connected to the new University of Hawai?i AI Institute currently under development. The Institute’s mission is to advance human-first AI through research, education, and service that strengthen the capabilities and well-being of Hawai?i’s people, with human judgment and decision-making, responsible stewardship, and community-defined priorities guiding its work. Research Focus We seek candidates who advance the foundations, methods, and computing systems needed for safe, trustworthy, resilient, and human-first AI. Areas of interest include AI safety and alignment; security and privacy of AI models, privacy-preserving AI systems; verification, validation, and monitoring; dependable embedded and edge AI; resilient cyber-physical and intelligent sensing systems; and human-centered evaluation and responsible deployment. Contributions may address ethical, legal, and societal implications of AI and malicious attacks, failures, uncertainty, or resource constraints through AI methods, enabling computing systems, or their integration. Duties and Responsibilities Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise and related areas of artificial intelligence, as well as other courses in information and computer sciences Establish or sustain an extramurally funded research program; publish high-quality scholarly work Supervise graduate research Mentor and advise undergraduate Participate in capstone mentoring; develop interdisciplinary and external collaborations Provide department, college, and university service; and Perform other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date. Evidence of high-quality research through peer-reviewed publications in conferences or journals relevant to the position sought. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and advising. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with diverse groups of students, faculty, and external partners. For Assistant Professor appointments: strong potential to establish an independent research program and obtain extramural funding. For Associate Professor consideration: an established independent research program, sustained scholarly contributions and extramural funding, and a record of effective teaching, student mentoring, and service appropriate to the rank. Desirable Qualifications Experience developing or evaluating AI methods or computing systems relevant to this position, including efficient AI computing, agents that plan and use tools, or systems operating under safety, security, reliability, privacy, or resource constraints. Experience translating research into usable tools, models, software, platforms, benchmarks, or evaluation methods, with attention to accessibility, meaningful human control, and impacts on users and communities. Experience with interdisciplinary or applied research involving government, industry, or nonprofit partners. Experience mentoring students from a variety of backgrounds and disciplines through research, internships, or collaborative projects. About the University The University of Hawai?i at Manoa is the flagship campus of the UH system and the only R1 university in the state. It is dedicated to teaching, research, and service in a multicultural and inclusive environment. Appointment Conditions Pending position clearance. This is an Assistant or Associate Professor search; rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and subject to applicable University appointment requirements. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. How to Apply Apply through the posting for this position and submit the following materials with your application: A cover letter identifying this position and explaining how you satisfy the relevant minimum and desirable qualifications. Candidates applying to multiple searches may also indicate their interest in the other positions. A curriculum vitae with a publication list. Names and contact information for three individuals willing to provide professional reference letters. A statement of research interests, accomplishments, and future research plans. A statement describing your experience and approach to teaching and mentoring, including any experience mentoring projects with industry or other external partners. Candidates whose expertise spans more than one area are encouraged to apply to each relevant position and indicate that interest in their cover letter. Applications will be evaluated separately against the qualifications of each position. Where permitted, interviews and campus visits may be coordinated across searches. Application materials will not be returned. Incomplete applications will not be accepted. Inquiries: Guylaine Poisson, guylaine@hawaii.edu (Department Chair) EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5533511/educational-specialist-american-sign-language-interpreter-training-program-po": {
      "title": "Educational Specialist, (American Sign Language Interpreter Training Program) (pos.0096871T)",
      "description": "Educational Specialist, (American Sign Language Interpreter Training Program) (pos.0096871T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02309 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - CALL - Department of Linguistics Opening Date 10/28/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096871T Description Title: Educational Specialist (American Sign Language Interpreter Training Program) Position Number: 0096871T Hiring Unit: College of Arts, Languages and Letters, Linguistics Department Location: University of Hawaii at Manoa Date Posted: October 28, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 Band: B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Month: 11-Month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities *Serves as the lead to launch and administer a bachelor-level American Sign Language (ASL) Interpreter Training Program (ITP). *Conducts research on established ASL ITPs to gather information for a program model tailored to higher education needs that serve students on multiple islands. Presents findings and strategic recommendations to the department, college leadership and community stakeholders to guide program design decisions. *Manages the administrative logistics for curriculum proposals and processes course actions. *Establishes and coordinates administrative requirements for new and expanded concentrations in ASL and Sign Language Interpretation. *Tracks ASL ITP data; monitors program data and maintains databases regarding enrollments, student performance, and progress. *Serves as an advisor to assist students with program requirements, course offerings, orientation, and campus navigation. *Advises students on the transition from language learning to professional interpreting, including necessary credentials, licensure disclosure, and national standards for interpreter candidates. *Plans, coordinates, and implements new student orientation sessions. *Acts as the program's primary point of contact for responding to inquiries, disseminating information, and assisting with faculty recruitments. *Plans, coordinates, and implements recruitment activities and special academic events to promote the department's undergraduate and graduate offerings. *Cultivates and maintains strategic partnerships with interpretation-related community organizations, interpreting agencies, and state agencies to support student recruitment, secure practicum and mentorship opportunities, and drive workforce development initiatives. *Facilitates requests for extramural funding to support the ITP, including renewal of funding. *Resolves operational, academic, and administrative matters effectively in accordance with current policies and procedures. *Keeps abreast of federal, state, and university policies and professional standards governing ASL interpretation, language access, and academic research Other duties as assigned *Denotes essential functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Sign Language Interpretation, Deaf Studies, Linguistics or a related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for program management, academic advising, administrative coordination, or sign language interpretation; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices, and techniques in the area of interpretation of ASL as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range and pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with higher education, student academic services, or disability and communication access for d/Deaf, Hard-of-Hearing, and Deaf-Blind individuals. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problem in an effective and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employees relations. Demonstrated expertise in ASL interpretation, including comprehensive knowledge of the interpreting profession, training pathways (ITPs), and local and national credentialing systems. Proven experience working closely with Deaf communities and a demonstrated ability to network within the professional interpreting field to promote students and programs. Demonstrated ability to manage, organize, analyze, and share program data for tracking and reporting purposes. Ability to work occasional nights and weekends for program-related events and activities. Desirable Qualifications Master's degree in Sign Language Interpretation, Deaf Studies, Linguistics, or a related field. Experience in website maintenance and using social media for public relations. Experience planning and executing promotional campaigns, outreach strategies, or recruitment events targeted at prospective students or community stakeholders. Fluency in ASL. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following required documents: A cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, Current resume, Official transcripts (copies acceptable for application, but if selected, originals required upon hire), Names of three (3) professional references (including phone number and e-mail addresses). Note: Please create a new account if this is your first time applying for a position using NEOGOV. Inquiries: Amy Schafer, Linguistics Department Chair, lingdc@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5533796/graduate-research-assistant-0000ga11": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11)",
      "description": "Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02312 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Thompson School of Social Work & Public Health - DSW Opening Date 10/28/2026 Closing Date 11/10/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0000GA11 Description Title: Graduate Research Assistant (0000GA11) Position Number: 0000GA11 Hiring Unit: Department of Social Work Location: University of Hawai'i at Manoa Date Posted: October 28, 2026 Closing Date: November 10, 2026, 11:59 PM Hawai'i Time Salary Information: GA Step 17 (11-month) or comparable to qualifications and experience, see GA Compensation Schedule Monthly Type: 11-month Full Time Equivalency (FTE): .50 Job Term: Non-Tenure/Part-Time/Temporary Funding: RTRF Other Conditions: pending position clearance and availability of funds. position is only available for one year. position to tentatively begin in December 2026 or soon thereafter. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist the principal investigator with coordination and implementation of the Improving Mobile Crisis Response in Honolulu County Study. Assist with communication and coordination with project partners including the Hawai'i Community Safety Coalition and the Hawai'i Health and Harm Reduction Center. Conduct a literature review on mobile crisis response. Support the recruitment of focus group participants. Coordinate focus groups including scheduling with participants, confirming space, setting up and breaking down Co-facilitate focus groups when needed. Assist with data transcription and data analysis. Assist with writing the final report and journal article. Assist with the dissemination of the final report. Participate in meetings, zoom calls, and trainings for the project. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Classified graduate student at the University of Hawai'i at Manoa. Be in good academic standing and maintain a GPA of 3.0 or above. Maintain 6 credits per semester, with the exception of those who have reached 800 (dissertation) or 700F (thesis). Able to work respectfully with diverse communities, and with a heightened level of sensitivity necessary for mental health/suicide prevention work. Able to work and time-manage independently, under direction of the principal investigator and project team, and with appropriate training. Prior experience conducting literature searches via online academic search engines (e.g., Pubmed, Psycinfo, etc.). Working knowledge of computers and basic processing (e.g., Microsoft Suite including Word, Excel, Powerpoint) and connectivity programs (e.g., Zoom, Webex, Teams). Must have strong skills with respect to communication (oral and written), organization, and attention-to-detail. Have access to transportation for visits/meetings with community sites. Must be personally ready/able to work in suicide prevention (those who have recently been impacted by suicide loss, or are personally struggling, sometimes find it difficult to engage in suicide prevention work). Desirable Qualifications: University of Hawai'i graduate student in public health, social work, psychology, or related field. Knowledge or experience in the fields of community safety and crisis response. Prior experience writing reports and/or peer-reviewed manuscripts. Prior experience on projects that required interfacing with diverse populations, community partners, and the general public. Able to commit to project for at least one year. To Apply: Click on the \"APPLY\" button to complete an application and attach the following REQUIRED documents/information: current curriculum vitae; cover letter describing how you meet the minimum and desirable qualifications; names and contact information (including email addresses) of at least 3 professional references; and transcripts or equivalent documents from institution confirming highest educational degree relevant to this position (unofficial copies acceptable, official originals required upon hire ). Note: First time NEOGOV users are required to create an account. Only applications through NEOGOV are accepted. Inquiries: Dr. Cameron W. Rasmussen, Principal Investigator, cwras@hawaii,edu . EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5535216/assistant-h3-associate-professor-h4-uhpc-core-faculty-88873t-86735t": {
      "title": "Assistant (H3)/Associate Professor (H4) (UHPC Core Faculty - 88873T & 86735T)",
      "description": "Assistant (H3)/Associate Professor (H4) (UHPC Core Faculty - 88873T & 86735T) Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02325 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Medicine Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 88873T & 86735T Description Title: Assistant (H3) / Associate Professor (H4) (UHPC Core Faculty) Position #: 88873T & 86735T (2 positions) Hiring Unit: Department of Medicine Location: Queen’s University Tower Campus Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous – application review begins Tuesday, November 17, 2026 Salary Information: Salary commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 11 Month Tenure Track: Non-Tenure Full/Part- Time: Part Time – Full Time equivalency (FTE): up to 10% Tenure/Non-Tenure: Non-Tenure Temp/Perm: Temporary Other Conditions: First review of applications to begin November 2026. Annually renewable subject to satisfactory performance and funding availability, to begin approximately December 2026. Duties & Responsibilities: Supports the Department Chair and University of Hawaii Primary Care and Internal Medicine (UHPCIM) Program Director by assisting with curriculum development, implementation, and evaluation, as well as faculty and organizational development. Ensures development aligns with educational principles, accreditation requirements, and the University’s objectives. Mentors advanced medical students, interns, and residents. Participates in curriculum development, implementation, and evaluation, as well as in faculty and organizational development. Helps develop and evaluate a Primary Care Curriculum for UHPCIM residents and medical students. May serve on committees as agreed upon by the Department Chair/UHPCIM Program Director. Other scholarly duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Assistant Professor MD degree or equivalent medical degree from an accredited medical school. Completion of an appropriate residency and/or fellowship training program approved by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) or the American Osteopathic Association (AOA). Board certification or qualification to become board certified in Internal Medicine through the American Board of Internal Medicine. Valid Hawaii Medical license by date of hire. Demonstrated ability in teaching and patient care. Professionalism in meeting and conferring with others. Organizational skills and leadership abilities. Associate Professor Assistant Professor plus: Four years in the rank of Assistant Professor, or four years of other medical experience, or a total of four years of teaching in the rank of Assistant Professor and other experience, or one year at the rank of Associate Professor at another medical school of recognized standing. Board certification in Internal Medicine through the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) and recertification when appropriate. Evidence of excellent patient care in a collaborative environment. Evidence of effective research and scholarly activity (i.e., recent publications in peer- reviewed journals or regional/national presentations, quality initiatives, or medical education administration. Experience in academic and/or clinical program administration. Familiarity with Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME) and ACGME requirements. Desirable Qualifications: Demonstrated experience with curriculum development and evaluation. Eligibility for clinical privileges at The Queen’s Health. Familiarity with curriculum development and evaluation, provider of educational activities as required by the LCME, ACGME, and the Accreditation Council for Continuing Medical Education (ACCME). To apply: Click on the “ Apply ” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, Curriculum Vitae, Names and contact information of five professional references, Copy of U.S. Board Certification AND current U.S. medical license or official transcripts from medical school plus certificates of completion from residency training and current U.S. medical license. Copies of transcripts are accepted when used instead of current U.S. board certification or current U.S. medical license. However, if used for verification, original transcripts are required upon hire. The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Inquiries: Scott K. Kuwada, MD – skkuwada@hawaii.edu Maria Inong - minong@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5535241/assistant-instructor-bs-nursing-program-0082718-0084959-2-posns": {
      "title": "Assistant Instructor (BS Nursing Program) 0082718, 0084959 (2 posns)",
      "description": "Assistant Instructor (BS Nursing Program) 0082718, 0084959 (2 posns) Salary $0.00 Semi-Monthly Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02327 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene - Nursing Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0082718, 0084959 Description Title: Assistant Instructor (BS Nursing Program) Position Number: 0082718, 0084959 (2 positions) Hiring Unit: School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene Location: Manoa Campus / Honolulu Date Posted: October 28, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment until filled. 1st review of applications will begin on November 20, 2026; 2nd review is on December 7, 2026. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience. Monthly Type: 9 Month Tenure Track: Non-Tenure Eligible Full Time/Part Time: Full Time, 100% Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: The UHM School of Nursing and Dental Hygiene (SONDH) invites qualified applicants for two (2) non-tenure eligible, full-time, 9-month faculty positions. The position is expected to begin this Spring Semester 2027 or as mutually acceptable. Renewal of appointment is dependent on performance, program needs and/or availability of funds. To learn more about SONDH, go to: http://www.nursing.hawaii.edu/ . Duties & Responsibilities: Teach assigned undergraduate and pre-licensure courses in the Department of Nursing. Specific priorities of need include adult health, psychiatric mental health, pediatric, and obstetric nursing. Participate in curriculum development activities as appropriate or assigned. Participate in didactic, lab practicum and clinical settings. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Master's degree in Nursing from a college or university of recognized standing. Current unrestricted RN license to practice in Hawai'i. Minimum of one (1) year of full-time work experience in the area of nursing specialty. Engaged in professional practice with a similar patient population and level of patient acuity where they will be assigned for the purpose of supervising nursing students. Demonstrated ability to teach effectively, provide clinical supervision, provide student feedback, and direct group discussion. Demonstrated ability to effectively meet, interact and confer with others. Demonstrated competency in interprofessional team work and team science Desirable Qualifications: Prior teaching experience. Experience in curriculum development and evaluation. Certified or eligible for certification in advanced practice. To Apply: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; Resume; Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of three (3) professional references; and Copy of applicable unofficial transcripts (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries and copy of diplomas are NOT ACCEPTABLE. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. NOTE: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the last one will be reviewed. Please do not include any self-identifying photos they will be redacted. PLEASE REDACT Social Security #s and birth dates on transcript copies. All submitted items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. No late applications will be accepted. Inquiries: Corie O. Fulgencio-Arre, HR Specialist; mcfa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534958/casual-hire-admin-fiscal-support-spec": {
      "title": "Casual Hire - Admin & Fiscal Support Spec",
      "description": "Casual Hire - Admin & Fiscal Support Spec Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02319 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/2/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire - Admin & Fiscal Support Spec Hiring Unit: Student-Athlete Success Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 2, 2026 Temporary/Permanent: Temporary, Full-time Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, 89 day hire, not to exceed 40 hours/week. Duties and Responsibilities Under the supervision of the Associate Athletics Director for Student-Athlete Services, provides administrative support to the Associate Athletics Director on a wide variety of administrative responsibilities and works directly with coaching staff of assigned sports. Provides office management including but not limited to establishing office procedures, scheduling meetings, greeting/screening guests, determining disposition of incoming correspondence/mail, preparing documents, and developing/maintaining an effective filing system. Coordinates and ensures that team and recruiting travel arrangements are prepared, completed and meet coach and team requirements (e.g. scheduling, booking hotel accommodations, and event logistics). Responsible for preparation of travel documents for assigned sports and programs. Prepares requisitions, reconciles and completes documents upon traveler¿s return. Ensures compliance with UH policies and procedures and NCAA rules and regulations. Assists with the review of the propriety of travel requests, requests for additional funding, etc. Assists with the review, analysis and preparation of administrative, fiscal and personnel related documents as requested by the Associate Athletics Director. Ensures compliance with UH and UH Foundation fiscal policies and procedures. Coordinates Associate Athletic Director¿s master calendar and related arrangements. Schedules appointments and meetings, coordinates and maintains calendars, makes arrangements for travel and accommodation and processes travel expense reports. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Maintains collaborative working relationships with administrators and coaches in the department, other college offices, and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, and other individuals interested in the Athletics Department. Knows, recognizes and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawai'i, its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated Conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Athletics Director or designee if there are reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Arts and Sciences, Business Administration, Humanities, Social Sciences, Natural Sciences, Education or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for program administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of program administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with program administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of program administration. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and spreadsheet software. Ability to exercise discretion and sound analyses to a variety of administrative support, program management or related issues. Ability to work effectively and independently under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Must be able to work a flexible schedule including nights, weekends and holidays, depending on programmatic needs. Ability to handle confidential information with a high level of tact, discretion and diplomacy. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number). Inquiries : Laura Beeman, Associate Athletics Director for Student-Athlete Success, lbeeman@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534920/casual-hire-assistant-mens-tennis-coach": {
      "title": "Casual Hire - Assistant Men’s Tennis Coach",
      "description": "Casual Hire - Assistant Men’s Tennis Coach Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02318 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/2/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire - Assistant Men’s Tennis Coach Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Men’s Tennis Program Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 2, 2026 Full Time / Part Time: Part time, Temporary Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, hours not to exceed 19.75 hours per week. Continuous recruitment until programmatic needs are met. Duties and Responsibilities: Assists with all aspects of NCAA Division I men’s tennis program, including student recruitment and development, compliance with NCAA, Conference and University of Hawai‘i rules and regulations, student-athlete academic achievement, and other duties assigned by the Head Coach. Assists and participates in practices and intercollegiate competitions. Teach necessary skills from the basic fundamentals to the techniques and strategies. Promotes and fosters a positive team environment, appropriate behavior, and sportsmanship by team members. Responsible for the conduct of team members both on and off the court. Promotes and maintains an environment where the physical, mental, social and academic well-being of all student-athletes are a priority. Assists with evaluating, recruiting and selecting qualified prospects that have the ability to compete in the classroom and athletically. Actively seek out and encourage a diverse group of prospects and assist them through the application process. Work collaboratively with the Compliance, Student-Athlete Services, and Academics staff to qualify and process prospects in a timely and efficient manner. Make recurring contacts by telephone, written correspondence, off-campus visits and evaluations, and coordination of campus visits as assigned by the Head Coach. Assists with overseeing the academic progress and success of student-athletes including promoting and maintaining an environment that encourages academic achievement as measured by academic eligibility, retention, and graduation. Actively participates in promotional and public relation activities for the men’s tennis program. Responsible for performing the duties of a Campus Security Authority as defined by University policy and federal law with regards to the Clery Act. Maintains an effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Knows, recognizes, and complies with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawaii and its employees, and the rules of the National Collegiate Athletic Association and of the affiliated conference as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if you have reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees or students, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Graduation from an accredited four year college or university; Some experience coaching tennis at the college or university level; Knowledge of men’s tennis as competed at the collegiate level or/and at the professional level. Strong oral and written communication and presentation skills. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Ability to work evenings, weekends, holidays and travel Ability to work cooperatively and effectively with people of diverse backgrounds. Computer, cell phone, and social media technology competency. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Required to possess and maintain a valid driver’s license. First Aid, CPR and AED certification at the time of employment or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Desirable Qualification s: Division I men’s tennis coaching experience or professional playing experience. Ability to understand and work effectively in Hawai‘i’s multi-cultural society. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number), and 4) certification. Inquiries : Hendrik Bode, Head Men’s Tennis Coach, hbode@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534984/casual-hire-athletics-equipment-manager": {
      "title": "Casual Hire - Athletics Equipment Manager",
      "description": "Casual Hire - Athletics Equipment Manager Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02321 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/2/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees Title: Casual Hire - Athletics Equipment Manager Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Equipment Room Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing date: November 2, 2026 Full Time / Part Time: Full-Time, temporary Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, 89 day hire, not to exceed 40 hours/week. Hours may vary. Duties and Responsibilities Responsible for providing student-athletes equipment, supplies, and accessories as needed and ensures its worthiness to safeguard each individual by systematic maintenance and repair of equipment and supplies. Responsible for training and supervising student-assistants ensuring the Equipment Room is clean and orderly. Help setting up and storing equipment for sports programs practices. Laundering of sports apparel and uniforms after practice and event. Responsible for securing equipment room after normal working hours and event. Maintain a collaborative and positive working relationships with administrators, coaches, student-athletes, vendors, and other college offices, and appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, and other individuals interested in the sports programs. Maintains confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawaii policies. Know, recognize, and comply with the laws, policies, rules, and regulations governing the University of Hawai‘i and its employees, and the rules of the National Collegiate Athletic Association and of the affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there is reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees, students, or others have occurred or will occur. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate degree. Some experience working in a collegiate athletic equipment room or equivalent combination of education and experience. Ability to lift athletic equipment and supplies weighing up to 50 pounds. Ability to work effectively and under pressure, with frequent interruptions and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Excellent organizational and time management skills. Must be able to travel with the sports team(s) as required. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver’s license (class 3) or comparable driver’s license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, and 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number) . Inquiries : Alan Ginoza, ginozaa@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534969/casual-hire-assistant-strength-and-conditioning-coordinator": {
      "title": "Casual Hire – Assistant Strength and Conditioning Coordinator",
      "description": "Casual Hire – Assistant Strength and Conditioning Coordinator Location UH Manoa - Athletics Dept, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02320 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Athletics Department Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/2/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: casual Description ****Applicants seeking short-term or part-time work with the University Hawai'i may apply for Casual Hire positions, which include administrative, professional, and technical (APT) or instructional-type positions. Because of the temporary nature of these appointments, Casual Hire employees are not eligible for most benefits as provided to regular BOR and Civil Service employees such as paid leave and holidays. Casual Hire appointments may last for up to 89 days for full-time employees, and up to 1 calendar year for part-time employees. Title: Casual Hire – Assistant Strength and Conditioning Coordinator Hiring Unit: UHM Athletics Location: Strength and Conditioning Date Posted : October 29, 2026 Closing Date : November 2, 2026 Full Time / Part Time : Temporary Full-Time Other Conditions: Position is a temporary casual hire position and offers no benefits, hours not to exceed 40 hours per week. 89 day appointment. Duties and Responsibilities: Responsible for developing, implementing and monitoring a safe and effective strength and conditioning program to reduce injuries and improve performance for all assigned sports. Provide in season, off season and pre-season strength and conditioning programs for all assigned sports. Responsible for completion and distribution of summer programs to assigned sports. Programs must include strength training, conditioning, nutrition, pre testing, illustrations, diagrams and performance goals for the summer training cycle. Works closely with coaches to develop team and individual programs based on the latest techniques in assigned sports. Responsible for coordinating staff coverage and provides daily supervision of student-athletes and student assistants in the weight room. Responsible for maintaining all strength and conditioning equipment through normal maintenance and cleaning. Instructs and provides information concerning strength training which includes nutritional counseling, rehabilitation training, and individual strength programs. Educates student-athletes about the risks involved on the use of illegal drugs and alcohol. If needed, student-athlete will be referred to counseling and treatment referral services. Keeps abreast of new techniques, methods and practices to consistently ensure and maintain that the strength and conditioning program meets standards. Responsible for developing and providing physical skills testing for student-athletes of assigned sports. Works closely with the Certified Athletic Trainers to modify training programs to accommodate student-athletes in rehabilitation. Flexible to work on weekends, nights and holidays as needed to accommodate teams, sporting events, and recruits visiting campus. Travel with team(s) as assigned to regular and/or post-season competition. Assure that all activities are operated in compliance with NCAA, Conference and university rules and regulations. Maintains collaborative working relationships w/administrators & coaches in the department, other college offices, & appropriate relationships with members of the media, donors, & other individuals interested in the Athletics Department & assigned sport programs. Maintain confidentiality to include but not limited to information, files and records that the incumbent may obtain or possess, in accordance with federal and state laws and University of Hawai‘i policies. Responsible for performing the duties of a Campus Security Authority as defined by University policy and federal law with regards to the Clery Act. Know, recognize and comply with the laws, policies, rules and regulations governing the University of Hawai‘i and its employees, and the rules of the NCAA and affiliated conferences as now constituted or as may be amended in the future. Immediately advises the Director of Athletics or designee if there is reasonable cause to believe violations, whether by employees or students, have occurred or will occur. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Exercise Science or related field and 2 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for intercollegiate athletics strength and conditioning program; or equivalent education/training or experience. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the athletics strength and conditioning programs as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with athletics strength and conditioning programs. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. For supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Valid certification by the National Strength and Conditioning Association (NSCA), Certified Strength and Conditioning Specialist (CSCS), Collegiate Strength and Conditioning Coaches Association (CSCCa) or Strength and Conditioning Coach (SCCC). Must be able to work nights, weekends and holidays during athletic related scheduled events and practices. Valid First Aid/CPR/AED certification within 30 days of hire. Ability to meet or exceed customer service needs and expectations and provide excellent service in a direct or indirect manner. Ability to work effectively and independently under pressure, with frequent interruptions, and remain flexible in handling several concurrent projects with competing deadlines. Ability to travel with the team 3-7 days per trip. Any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Desirable Qualifications: United States of America Weightlifting Association (USAW) certification. Experience training collegiate football & Olympic sports. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach 1) cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2) resume, and 3) names of 3 professional references (include title, email and phone number) 4) .valid certification by the National Strength and Conditioning Association (NSCA), Certified Strength and Conditioning Specialist (CSCS), Collegiate Strength and Conditioning Coaches Association (CSCCa) or Strength and Conditioning Coach (SCCC). Inquiries: : Thomas Heffernan, theffern@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5534629/operations-coordinator-0097757": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator (0097757)",
      "description": "Operations Coordinator (0097757) Location UH Manoa, Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02315 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - Chancellor's Office Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097757 Description Title: Operations Coordinator Position Number: 0097757 Hiring Unit: OFFICE OF THE CHANCELLOR Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Provides administrative and operational assistance to the Office of the Chancellor, supporting daily office functions and facilitating efficient administrative processes. Reports directly to the Chief Administrative Specialist to the Chancellor. 2. *Manages scheduling, logistical details, and related arrangements for campus visits, institutional meetings, and executive-level engagements. 3. *Oversees the progression of high-priority campus requests, strategic initiatives, and institutional directives from assignment to completion; initiates and enforces follow-up actions across campus units to ensure executive deadlines are met. 4. *Gathers data and prepares special, nonrecurring written reports, briefing summaries, and project tracking logs for senior management regarding office workflows and ongoing campus initiatives. 5. *Evaluates and develops improved daily office procedures, including document management workflows, routing systems for incoming correspondence, and executive meeting protocols to keep the office running smoothly. 6. *Advises the Chief Administrative Specialist to the Chancellor on project timelines, flags potential administrative delays or scheduling conflicts, and recommends practical solutions to keep tasks on track. 7. *Independently investigates and resolves routine operational and administrative matters, applying a clear understanding of University policies and executive directives to address questions from internal and external stakeholders. 8. *Provides clear guidance and administrative support to colleges, program offices, and campus personnel regarding the Chancellor's Office protocols and processing timelines. 9. Manages fiscal accountability and purchasing documentation for the unit, including p-card transactions, purchase orders, and equipment/supply inventory in accordance with University policies and regulations. 10. Supervises, trains, and schedules student assistants, setting clear performance expectations to maintain a professional, secure, and confidential environment. 11. Other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential functions Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Communications, Social Sciences, or a related field and 3 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for office management, operations coordination, or executive administrative support; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of office administration as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with office administration. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and proficiency using Microsoft Office applications (Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint) and Google Workspace applications (Docs, Sheets, Drive, Gmail, and Calendar). 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Demonstrated ability to handle highly confidential and sensitive information with discretion, integrity, and sound judgment. 10. Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment in determining the appropriate handling, sharing, retention, and safeguarding of confidential information. 11. Demonstrated ability to represent an executive office with professionalism, tact, diplomacy, and discretion in communications and interactions with internal and external constituents. Desirable Qualifications 1. Experience working in a higher education environment providing direct operational support within an executive or central administrative office. 2. Familiarity with the University of Hawai'i system structure, campus operations, and applicable institutional administrative rules and policies. 3. Ability to work early mornings, nights, weekends and holidays, as needed, to support Chancellor's priorities and campus events. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicants must submit the following: 1. Cover lette r indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications; 2. Resume or CV; 3. Names and contact information (telephone number and email addresses) of 3 professional references ; 4. Copy of unofficial transcript (official transcripts will be required at the time of hire). Academic record/grade summaries will NOT be accepted. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted.) Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. IMPORTANT: If multiple applications are received from the same candidate, only the Last one will be reviewed. Please DO NOT include any self-identifying photos, REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE on any documents within the application packet. All items become the property of the University of Hawaii and will not be returned. Only complete applications will be passed on to the search committee for consideration. Incomplete or unreadable application materials will not be considered. Inquiries: Michelle Isa-Atta, 808-956-8456 isam@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5495116/readvertisement-admin-fiscal-support-specialist-0096970t": {
      "title": "READVERTISEMENT - Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0096970T)",
      "description": "READVERTISEMENT - Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist (0096970T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - JABSOM, Queen's Medical Center - Honolulu, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02048 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Division Psychiatry Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/16/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096970T Description READVERTISEMENT Title: Admin & Fiscal Support Specialist Position Number: 0096970T Hiring Unit: C OF HLTH SCI & SW, SCH OF MED, PSYCHIATRY DEPT Location: Queens Medical Center Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: November 16, 2026 at 11:59 PM HST Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBA, minimum $4,627/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Temporary, full-time, subject to position clearance and availability of funds. Position is annually renewable depending on performance and/or funding availability. Duties and Responsibilities *Provides administrative, fiscal, research, and educational support activities. *Performs procurement and fiscal actions, including the preparation of purchase orders, requisitions, invitations to bid, processes travel requests, p-card reallocation, requests for proposals/quotations or proposal/bid/technical specifications. Reviews and ensures the appropriateness and correctness of travel documents and processes for payment. Ensures that all goods and services are purchased in compliance with Federal, State and University guidelines and procedures. *Assist in personnel actions such as recruitment, on-boarding, and tracking of training and certification compliance, including for community providers/faculty non-compensated/volunteers. *Perform monthly reconciliation of project expenditures; generate monthly supporting documents ensuring compliance with funding agency's requirements. Maintain accurate account of project budget information and expenditures, generate financial report spreadsheets as necessary. Assist in processing and pursing grant and contracts. Assists with the preparation of a variety of reports. Anticipates, recognizes & resolves problems relating to the coordination of key management, operational, planning forums/meetings for the Department. Prepare and edit reports, agendas, letters, and complex documents such as quarterly reports that require specific formatting; and gather, interpret, and analyze information. Work with multiple agencies and affiliated partners at various locations. Supervision of staff, student hires, and/or volunteers as needed. Provides general office support in establishing and maintaining files, records and documents; answers general inquiries. *Coordinates continuing medical education (CME) activities as directed. Coordinates meetings/schedules and takes minutes. Performs other duties as assigned. * Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications Possession of baccalaureate degree in administration, business, education, psychology, healthcare, or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area(s) of business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, personnel, etc.), educational administration, and/or research administration. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. Proven ability to quickly master complex concepts and methodologies, as demonstrated through academic coursework, projects, or self-directed study. Strong academic foundation with a proven capacity to learn new systems, tools, and processes with minimal guidance. Functional knowledge of current or previous financial or HR systems. Ability to learn and apply guidelines, policies, and procedures. Demonstrated ability to multi-task; assess situations to prioritize tasks based on importance and deadlines. Ability to travel independently in a timely and efficient manner, to various locations. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications 2 years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for business administration (e.g., budget, fiscal, human resources/personnel, etc.), research administration, and/or educational administration; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the field of business, human resources, and/or fiscal demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Experience meeting requirements for multiple projects with multiple deadlines, which included effective organization, coordination, implementation, and communication. Experience working in human resources or business-related field performing administrative actions. Experience and working knowledge of the University of Hawai`i (UH) and the Research Corporation of the University of Hawai`i (RCUH) fiscal and human resources policies and procedures. Working experience with the University's electronic systems including but not limited to Kuali Financial System (KFS), RCUH Financial Portal, and Jasper Reporting Server (JRS). Experience using the UH myGRANT system. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents by the closing date: a cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications current resume official transcripts confirming degree completion (copies acceptable; however official transcripts sent directly from the degree granting institution required upon hire) Three (3) professional references (complete with contact information including e-mail address) The application will be considered incomplete if materials are unreadable. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Tiffinie Kiyota-Chan - KiyotaT@dop.hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - John A. Burns School of Medicine Address 651 Ilalo Street Honolulu, Hawaii, 96813 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5535151/uhcdc-program-specialist-pos-0081522t": {
      "title": "UHCDC Program Specialist (pos #0081522T)",
      "description": "UHCDC Program Specialist (pos #0081522T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Manoa - School of Arch, Honolulu, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02324 Department University of Hawai'i at Manoa - School of Architecture Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date 11/17/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0081522T Description Title: Program Specialist Position Number: 0081522T Hiring Unit: SCH OF ARCH, ARCH DEANS OFF, ARCH DEANS OFF Location: UH at Manoa Date Posted: 10/29/2026 Closing Date: 11/17/2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Part-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Duties and Responsibilities 1. *Provides program support to ensure achievement and advancement of School of Architecture (SOA) and University of Hawaii Community Design Center (UHCDC) mission and goals and to ensure professional, effective, and efficient daily operations. 2. *Works closely with the center stakeholders to identify and advance strategic priorities and initiatives for the center, including providing leadership and project management for special projects and programs. Recommends, communicates, and coordinates appropriate priorities across relevant parties, tracks and follows up on pending items, and moves priorities forward. 3. *Provides project management support to Director and PIs as applicable and supported by project contracts. Support may include design, programming, communications, and other technical functions as needed. 4. *Develops written and graphic material for outreach and business development to communicate data or other event information through reports, newsletters, social media posts, website, posters, and other media. 5. *Researches and develops assessment tools such as entry and exit surveys and collects and analyzes data related to program operations and administrative functions in coordination with the Director and Administrative Officer. 6. *Provides recommendations for program improvements or optimization. 7. *Supervises, coordinates workflow, directs tasks, and reviews work of student assistants as applicable to center-serving initiatives. 8. *Coordinates center wide data storage and sharing in accordance with University policies and procedures, collective bargaining agreements, and confidentiality requirements. Works with PIs and staff to ensure project data and products are securely maintained and stored per contract and UHCDC requirements. 9. *Participates in planning and executing events, visits, symposia, alumni events, and donor functions. 10. *Exercises the highest degree of discretion and confidentiality in administratively supporting the director on strategic, operational, and sensitive matters. 11. *Coordinates the documentation and management of equipment and software in accordance with University policies and procedures. 12. *Ensures general compliance with UH policies and procedures, collective bargaining agreements, SOA procedures, and other laws, rules, and regulations. 13. *Creates and maintains administrative records, including meeting minutes, administrative memos, and other archival materials. 14. *Works outside of the normal working hours, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and holidays, as directed for special events. Travels to various off-campus sites and/or geographical locations to attend or conduct meetings and workshops throughout the state and mainland, as required. 15. Other duties as assigned *Denotes Essential Functions Minimum Qualifications 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Environmental Design, Architecture, Planning or related field and three (3) year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for project management, administration of architecture, environmental, or community design related programs; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of environmental, architectural, or community design as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with program development. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software, such as Microsoft Suite, Google Suite, and other word and data processing software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Experience with communication and graphic design software programs including the Adobe Creative Suite (Photoshop, Illustrator, Indesign), web design (Squarespace), or other software used for presentations, newsletters, social media, or other communications. 10. Proven ability to translate complex information into clear, easy-to-understand visual and graphic presentations. 11. Ability to present effectively and engage with executive leadership, staff, external partners, and a variety of audiences. 12. Proven ability to maintain high professional standards. 13. Experience developing research, analysis and design documents. 14. Strong written and verbal communication skills. 15. Proficiency in discipline-relevant digital software applications including but not limited to Microsoft Office, Adobe Creative Cloud (including Illustrator), GIS, CAD/Revit, Rhinoceros, and Sketchup. 16. Experience with physical model modelmaking (using analog and digital tools). 17. Experience in designing, developing and conducting workshops or training. 18. Ability to work flexible working hours to meet program and participant needs. 19. Ability to travel independently to various geographical locations in a timely manner. Requirement may be fulfilled by a valid Hawaii Driver's license (class 3), comparable driver's license, or other means of transportation, which meets position needs. Desirable Qualifications 1. Recognized standing in the field with evidence of professional maturity as evidenced in project management experience. 2. Demonstrated ability to lead design outreach, education, and advocacy initiatives, for example through service to professional organizations or community design work. 3. Experience administering project budgets and timelines. 4. Experience in environmental, architectural, or community design. 5. Experience in the administration of programs in a university environment. 6. Experience in marketing and promoting initiatives and educational programs. 7. Knowledge of UH and RCUH policies & procedures for administering state or federally funded programs. 8. Experience in website design and maintenance. 9. Grant writing, fundraising, and/or administration experience. To Apply: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Applicants must submit the following: 1. Cover letter indicating how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications 2. CV/Resume 3. Official transcripts confirming degree completion (copies acceptable; however official transcripts sent directly from the degree granting institution required upon hire) 4. Three (3) professional references (complete with contact information including e-mail address) Inquiries: Contact Cathi Schar at cathi@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    }
  },
